[{"input":"# Paravectors in the algebra of physical space\n\nThe **algebra of physical space** (APS) starts with the geometry of ordinary three-dimensional Euclidean space and uses its Clifford algebra to describe both spatial geometry and relativistic spacetime. Its central construction is the **paravector**: a sum of a scalar and a spatial vector. A paravector is not a new basis vector in a four-dimensional Euclidean space. Rather, its scalar and vector parts together carry a natural Minkowski geometry.\n\nThis chapter develops that construction and its concrete realization using Pauli matrices. Prerequisites are vector dot and cross products, complex matrices, and elementary special relativity.\n\n## The underlying spatial algebra\n\nLet the orthonormal spatial basis vectors be ``e_1,e_2,e_3``. Their geometric product is associative and satisfies\n\n```math\ne_j e_k + e_k e_j = 2\\delta_{jk},\n\\qquad e_j^2=1.\n```\n\nDifferent basis vectors anticommute. Products such as ``e_1e_2`` are **bivectors**, representing oriented planes rather than spatial vectors. The real Clifford algebra ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}`` has eight basis elements:\n\n```math\n1,\\quad e_1,e_2,e_3,\\quad e_2e_3,e_3e_1,e_1e_2,\\quad I=e_1e_2e_3.\n```\n\nThe pseudoscalar ``I`` obeys ``I^2=-1`` and commutes with every element of this algebra. It therefore supplies a geometric imaginary unit. In particular,\n\n```math\ne_2e_3=I e_1,\\qquad e_3e_1=I e_2,\\qquad e_1e_2=I e_3.\n```\n\nFor spatial vectors ``\\mathbf a`` and ``\\mathbf b``, the geometric product combines their dot product and oriented plane:\n\n```math\n\\mathbf a\\mathbf b\n=\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b\n=\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+I(\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b).\n```\n\nThe cross-product formula assumes the right-handed orientation specified by ``I=e_1e_2e_3``. Unlike the cross product alone, the geometric product is associative.","output":"","result":"# Paravectors in the algebra of physical space\n\nThe **algebra of physical space** (APS) starts with the geometry of ordinary three-dimensional Euclidean space and uses its Clifford algebra to describe both spatial geometry and relativistic spacetime. Its central construction is the **paravector**: a sum of a scalar and a spatial vector. A paravector is not a new basis vector in a four-dimensional Euclidean space. Rather, its scalar and vector parts together carry a natural Minkowski geometry.\n\nThis chapter develops that construction and its concrete realization using Pauli matrices. Prerequisites are vector dot and cross products, complex matrices, and elementary special relativity.\n\n## The underlying spatial algebra\n\nLet the orthonormal spatial basis vectors be ``e_1,e_2,e_3``. Their geometric product is associative and satisfies\n\n```math\ne_j e_k + e_k e_j = 2\\delta_{jk},\n\\qquad e_j^2=1.\n```\n\nDifferent basis vectors anticommute. Products such as ``e_1e_2`` are **bivectors**, representing oriented planes rather than spatial vectors. The real Clifford algebra ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}`` has eight basis elements:\n\n```math\n1,\\quad e_1,e_2,e_3,\\quad e_2e_3,e_3e_1,e_1e_2,\\quad I=e_1e_2e_3.\n```\n\nThe pseudoscalar ``I`` obeys ``I^2=-1`` and commutes with every element of this algebra. It therefore supplies a geometric imaginary unit. In particular,\n\n```math\ne_2e_3=I e_1,\\qquad e_3e_1=I e_2,\\qquad e_1e_2=I e_3.\n```\n\nFor spatial vectors ``\\mathbf a`` and ``\\mathbf b``, the geometric product combines their dot product and oriented plane:\n\n```math\n\\mathbf a\\mathbf b\n=\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b\n=\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+I(\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b).\n```\n\nThe cross-product formula assumes the right-handed orientation specified by ``I=e_1e_2e_3``. Unlike the cross product alone, the geometric product is associative.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"chapter\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4765-paravectors-in-the-algebra-of-physical-space\">\n<h1>Paravectors in the algebra of physical space</h1>\n<p>The <strong>algebra of physical space</strong> (APS) starts with the geometry of ordinary three-dimensional Euclidean space and uses its Clifford algebra to describe both spatial geometry and relativistic spacetime. Its central construction is the <strong>paravector</strong>: a sum of a scalar and a spatial vector. A paravector is not a new basis vector in a four-dimensional Euclidean space. Rather, its scalar and vector parts together carry a natural Minkowski geometry.</p>\n<p>This chapter develops that construction and its concrete realization using Pauli matrices. Prerequisites are vector dot and cross products, complex matrices, and elementary special relativity.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4765-the-underlying-spatial-algebra\">\n<h1>The underlying spatial algebra</h1>\n<p>Let the orthonormal spatial basis vectors be <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_1,e_2,e_3\\)</span>. Their geometric product is associative and satisfies</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\ne_j e_k + e_k e_j = 2\\delta_{jk},\n\\qquad e_j^2=1.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Different basis vectors anticommute. Products such as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_1e_2\\)</span> are <strong>bivectors</strong>, representing oriented planes rather than spatial vectors. The real Clifford algebra <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\)</span> has eight basis elements:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n1,\\quad e_1,e_2,e_3,\\quad e_2e_3,e_3e_1,e_1e_2,\\quad I=e_1e_2e_3.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The pseudoscalar <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I\\)</span> obeys <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I^2=-1\\)</span> and commutes with every element of this algebra. It therefore supplies a geometric imaginary unit. In particular,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\ne_2e_3=I e_1,\\qquad e_3e_1=I e_2,\\qquad e_1e_2=I e_3.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For spatial vectors <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf a\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf b\\)</span>, the geometric product combines their dot product and oriented plane:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathbf a\\mathbf b\n=\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b\n=\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+I(\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The cross-product formula assumes the right-handed orientation specified by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I=e_1e_2e_3\\)</span>. Unlike the cross product alone, the geometric product is associative.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Exterior products, contractions, and duality\n\nWrite ``\\langle A\\rangle_r`` for the grade-``r`` part of a multivector. The underlying real vector space of APS is the exterior algebra of three-dimensional space, but Clifford multiplication also incorporates the Euclidean metric. For homogeneous elements ``A_r`` and ``B_s``, define\n\n```math\nA_r\\wedge B_s=\\langle A_rB_s\\rangle_{r+s},\n\\qquad\nA_r\\mathbin{\\lrcorner}B_s=\\langle A_rB_s\\rangle_{s-r}\\quad(r\\leq s).\n```\n\nThe left contraction is defined to be zero for ``r>s``. These conventions extend by linearity; wedge products of total grade above three vanish. Some texts insert a reversion in the definition of contraction, so signs must be compared before transferring formulas.\n\nFor vectors the symmetric and antisymmetric parts of the geometric product give\n\n```math\n\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b=\\frac12(\\mathbf a\\mathbf b+\\mathbf b\\mathbf a),\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b=\\frac12(\\mathbf a\\mathbf b-\\mathbf b\\mathbf a).\n```\n\nThe wedge product records oriented area without choosing a normal vector. In particular,\n\n```math\n\\mathbf a\\mathbin{\\lrcorner}(\\mathbf b\\wedge\\mathbf c)\n=(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)\\mathbf c-(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf c)\\mathbf b,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b\\wedge\\mathbf c\n=[\\mathbf a\\cdot(\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf c)]I.\n```\n\nFor example, ``e_1\\mathbin{\\lrcorner}(e_1\\wedge e_2)=e_2``, while ``e_2\\mathbin{\\lrcorner}(e_1\\wedge e_2)=-e_1``. The magnitude of a simple bivector obeys\n\n```math\n|\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b|^2\n=|\\mathbf a|^2|\\mathbf b|^2-(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)^2.\n```\n\n### An explicit duality convention\n\nDefine the Clifford dual by right multiplication with the inverse pseudoscalar:\n\n```math\nD(A)=AI^{-1}=-AI.\n```\n\nIt sends grade ``r`` to grade ``3-r`` and satisfies ``D^2(A)=-A``. For a bivector ``B=I\\mathbf b``, it gives ``D(B)=\\mathbf b``. Consequently,\n\n```math\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b=D(\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b),\n\\qquad\nD(e_1e_2)=e_3,\\qquad D(e_3)=-e_1e_2.\n```\n\nThis particular Clifford dual is not identical on every grade to the Euclidean Hodge star. With the chosen orientation, the latter satisfies\n\n```math\n\\star A_r=A_r^\\dagger I,\n```\n\nwhere the dagger reverses the order of geometric factors. For example, ``\\star e_3=e_1e_2`` and ``\\star(e_1e_2)=e_3``. Thus ``\\star^2=1`` in three-dimensional Euclidean space, unlike ``D^2=-1``. Reversing the orientation changes the pseudoscalar and the cross-product convention, but not the wedge product itself.\n\n### Exercises on exterior structure\n\n1. Derive the vector–bivector contraction identity by expanding the geometric products.\n2. Compute the wedge product of ``e_1+e_2`` and ``e_2+e_3`` and its Clifford dual. Check that the dual is perpendicular to both vectors.\n3. Verify the stated Hodge-star formula on all eight real basis elements.","output":"","result":"## Exterior products, contractions, and duality\n\nWrite ``\\langle A\\rangle_r`` for the grade-``r`` part of a multivector. The underlying real vector space of APS is the exterior algebra of three-dimensional space, but Clifford multiplication also incorporates the Euclidean metric. For homogeneous elements ``A_r`` and ``B_s``, define\n\n```math\nA_r\\wedge B_s=\\langle A_rB_s\\rangle_{r+s},\n\\qquad\nA_r\\mathbin{\\lrcorner}B_s=\\langle A_rB_s\\rangle_{s-r}\\quad(r\\leq s).\n```\n\nThe left contraction is defined to be zero for ``r>s``. These conventions extend by linearity; wedge products of total grade above three vanish. Some texts insert a reversion in the definition of contraction, so signs must be compared before transferring formulas.\n\nFor vectors the symmetric and antisymmetric parts of the geometric product give\n\n```math\n\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b=\\frac12(\\mathbf a\\mathbf b+\\mathbf b\\mathbf a),\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b=\\frac12(\\mathbf a\\mathbf b-\\mathbf b\\mathbf a).\n```\n\nThe wedge product records oriented area without choosing a normal vector. In particular,\n\n```math\n\\mathbf a\\mathbin{\\lrcorner}(\\mathbf b\\wedge\\mathbf c)\n=(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)\\mathbf c-(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf c)\\mathbf b,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b\\wedge\\mathbf c\n=[\\mathbf a\\cdot(\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf c)]I.\n```\n\nFor example, ``e_1\\mathbin{\\lrcorner}(e_1\\wedge e_2)=e_2``, while ``e_2\\mathbin{\\lrcorner}(e_1\\wedge e_2)=-e_1``. The magnitude of a simple bivector obeys\n\n```math\n|\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b|^2\n=|\\mathbf a|^2|\\mathbf b|^2-(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)^2.\n```\n\n### An explicit duality convention\n\nDefine the Clifford dual by right multiplication with the inverse pseudoscalar:\n\n```math\nD(A)=AI^{-1}=-AI.\n```\n\nIt sends grade ``r`` to grade ``3-r`` and satisfies ``D^2(A)=-A``. For a bivector ``B=I\\mathbf b``, it gives ``D(B)=\\mathbf b``. Consequently,\n\n```math\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b=D(\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b),\n\\qquad\nD(e_1e_2)=e_3,\\qquad D(e_3)=-e_1e_2.\n```\n\nThis particular Clifford dual is not identical on every grade to the Euclidean Hodge star. With the chosen orientation, the latter satisfies\n\n```math\n\\star A_r=A_r^\\dagger I,\n```\n\nwhere the dagger reverses the order of geometric factors. For example, ``\\star e_3=e_1e_2`` and ``\\star(e_1e_2)=e_3``. Thus ``\\star^2=1`` in three-dimensional Euclidean space, unlike ``D^2=-1``. Reversing the orientation changes the pseudoscalar and the cross-product convention, but not the wedge product itself.\n\n### Exercises on exterior structure\n\n1. Derive the vector–bivector contraction identity by expanding the geometric products.\n2. Compute the wedge product of ``e_1+e_2`` and ``e_2+e_3`` and its Clifford dual. Check that the dual is perpendicular to both vectors.\n3. Verify the stated Hodge-star formula on all eight real basis elements.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4768-exterior-products-contractions-and-duality\">\n<h1>Exterior products, contractions, and duality</h1>\n<p>Write <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\langle A\\rangle_r\\)</span> for the grade-<span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r\\)</span> part of a multivector. The underlying real vector space of APS is the exterior algebra of three-dimensional space, but Clifford multiplication also incorporates the Euclidean metric. For homogeneous elements <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A_r\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(B_s\\)</span>, define</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA_r\\wedge B_s=\\langle A_rB_s\\rangle_{r+s},\n\\qquad\nA_r\\mathbin{\\lrcorner}B_s=\\langle A_rB_s\\rangle_{s-r}\\quad(r\\leq s).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The left contraction is defined to be zero for <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r&gt;s\\)</span>. These conventions extend by linearity; wedge products of total grade above three vanish. Some texts insert a reversion in the definition of contraction, so signs must be compared before transferring formulas.</p>\n<p>For vectors the symmetric and antisymmetric parts of the geometric product give</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b=\\frac12(\\mathbf a\\mathbf b+\\mathbf b\\mathbf a),\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b=\\frac12(\\mathbf a\\mathbf b-\\mathbf b\\mathbf a).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The wedge product records oriented area without choosing a normal vector. In particular,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathbf a\\mathbin{\\lrcorner}(\\mathbf b\\wedge\\mathbf c)\n=(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)\\mathbf c-(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf c)\\mathbf b,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b\\wedge\\mathbf c\n=[\\mathbf a\\cdot(\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf c)]I.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For example, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_1\\mathbin{\\lrcorner}(e_1\\wedge e_2)=e_2\\)</span>, while <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_2\\mathbin{\\lrcorner}(e_1\\wedge e_2)=-e_1\\)</span>. The magnitude of a simple bivector obeys</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n|\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b|^2\n=|\\mathbf a|^2|\\mathbf b|^2-(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)^2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4768-an-explicit-duality-convention\">\n<h1>An explicit duality convention</h1>\n<p>Define the Clifford dual by right multiplication with the inverse pseudoscalar:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nD(A)=AI^{-1}=-AI.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>It sends grade <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r\\)</span> to grade <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(3-r\\)</span> and satisfies <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(D^2(A)=-A\\)</span>. For a bivector <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(B=I\\mathbf b\\)</span>, it gives <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(D(B)=\\mathbf b\\)</span>. Consequently,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b=D(\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b),\n\\qquad\nD(e_1e_2)=e_3,\\qquad D(e_3)=-e_1e_2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This particular Clifford dual is not identical on every grade to the Euclidean Hodge star. With the chosen orientation, the latter satisfies</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\star A_r=A_r^\\dagger I,\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>where the dagger reverses the order of geometric factors. For example, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\star e_3=e_1e_2\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\star(e_1e_2)=e_3\\)</span>. Thus <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\star^2=1\\)</span> in three-dimensional Euclidean space, unlike <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(D^2=-1\\)</span>. Reversing the orientation changes the pseudoscalar and the cross-product convention, but not the wedge product itself.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4768-exercises-on-exterior-structure\">\n<h1>Exercises on exterior structure</h1>\n<ol>\n<li>Derive the vector–bivector contraction identity by expanding the geometric products.</li>\n<li>Compute the wedge product of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_1+e_2\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_2+e_3\\)</span> and its Clifford dual. Check that the dual is perpendicular to both vectors.</li>\n<li>Verify the stated Hodge-star formula on all eight real basis elements.</li>\n</ol>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Paravectors and their Minkowski norm\n\nA real paravector is an element of the scalar-plus-vector subspace:\n\n```math\np=p^0+\\mathbf p\n=p^0+p^1e_1+p^2e_2+p^3e_3,\n\\qquad p^\\mu\\in\\mathbb R.\n```\n\nThis is a four-dimensional real subspace of the eight-dimensional algebra. For a spacetime event, take ``x=ct+\\mathbf x`` so that all components have units of length. For four-momentum, take ``p=E/c+\\mathbf p``.\n\nDefine **Clifford conjugation** by reversing the order of factors and changing the sign of each spatial vector. On a paravector it acts as\n\n```math\n\\bar p=p^0-\\mathbf p.\n```\n\nSince ``\\mathbf p^2=|\\mathbf p|^2``, the product with the conjugate is a real scalar:\n\n```math\np\\bar p=\\bar p p=(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2.\n```\n\nThis is the Minkowski quadratic form with signature ``(+,-,-,-)``. The minus signs arise from conjugation, not from making the spatial basis vectors square to minus one.\n\nThe associated bilinear inner product is\n\n```math\n\\langle p,q\\rangle_M\n=\\frac12\\left(p\\bar q+q\\bar p\\right)\n=p^0q^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf q.\n```\n\nA nonzero paravector is timelike, null, or spacelike when its squared norm is positive, zero, or negative, respectively. If the norm is nonzero, its algebraic inverse is\n\n```math\np^{-1}=\\frac{\\bar p}{p\\bar p}.\n```\n\nNonzero null paravectors have no inverse. Their failure to be invertible will become especially transparent in the matrix representation.\n\n!!! note \"Paravectors do not form a subalgebra\"\n\n    Adding paravectors gives another paravector, but multiplying them generally produces a bivector term. Computations therefore take place in the full spatial Clifford algebra, even when the quantities being represented are paravectors.","output":"","result":"## Paravectors and their Minkowski norm\n\nA real paravector is an element of the scalar-plus-vector subspace:\n\n```math\np=p^0+\\mathbf p\n=p^0+p^1e_1+p^2e_2+p^3e_3,\n\\qquad p^\\mu\\in\\mathbb R.\n```\n\nThis is a four-dimensional real subspace of the eight-dimensional algebra. For a spacetime event, take ``x=ct+\\mathbf x`` so that all components have units of length. For four-momentum, take ``p=E/c+\\mathbf p``.\n\nDefine **Clifford conjugation** by reversing the order of factors and changing the sign of each spatial vector. On a paravector it acts as\n\n```math\n\\bar p=p^0-\\mathbf p.\n```\n\nSince ``\\mathbf p^2=|\\mathbf p|^2``, the product with the conjugate is a real scalar:\n\n```math\np\\bar p=\\bar p p=(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2.\n```\n\nThis is the Minkowski quadratic form with signature ``(+,-,-,-)``. The minus signs arise from conjugation, not from making the spatial basis vectors square to minus one.\n\nThe associated bilinear inner product is\n\n```math\n\\langle p,q\\rangle_M\n=\\frac12\\left(p\\bar q+q\\bar p\\right)\n=p^0q^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf q.\n```\n\nA nonzero paravector is timelike, null, or spacelike when its squared norm is positive, zero, or negative, respectively. If the norm is nonzero, its algebraic inverse is\n\n```math\np^{-1}=\\frac{\\bar p}{p\\bar p}.\n```\n\nNonzero null paravectors have no inverse. Their failure to be invertible will become especially transparent in the matrix representation.\n\n!!! note \"Paravectors do not form a subalgebra\"\n\n    Adding paravectors gives another paravector, but multiplying them generally produces a bivector term. Computations therefore take place in the full spatial Clifford algebra, even when the quantities being represented are paravectors.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4764-paravectors-and-their-minkowski-norm\">\n<h1>Paravectors and their Minkowski norm</h1>\n<p>A real paravector is an element of the scalar-plus-vector subspace:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np=p^0+\\mathbf p\n=p^0+p^1e_1+p^2e_2+p^3e_3,\n\\qquad p^\\mu\\in\\mathbb R.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This is a four-dimensional real subspace of the eight-dimensional algebra. For a spacetime event, take <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=ct+\\mathbf x\\)</span> so that all components have units of length. For four-momentum, take <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(p=E/c+\\mathbf p\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Define <strong>Clifford conjugation</strong> by reversing the order of factors and changing the sign of each spatial vector. On a paravector it acts as</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\bar p=p^0-\\mathbf p.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf p^2=|\\mathbf p|^2\\)</span>, the product with the conjugate is a real scalar:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np\\bar p=\\bar p p=(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This is the Minkowski quadratic form with signature <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((+,-,-,-)\\)</span>. The minus signs arise from conjugation, not from making the spatial basis vectors square to minus one.</p>\n<p>The associated bilinear inner product is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\langle p,q\\rangle_M\n=\\frac12\\left(p\\bar q+q\\bar p\\right)\n=p^0q^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf q.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>A nonzero paravector is timelike, null, or spacelike when its squared norm is positive, zero, or negative, respectively. If the norm is nonzero, its algebraic inverse is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np^{-1}=\\frac{\\bar p}{p\\bar p}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Nonzero null paravectors have no inverse. Their failure to be invertible will become especially transparent in the matrix representation.</p>\n<aside data-type=\"note\">\n<h5>Paravectors do not form a subalgebra</h5>\n<p>Adding paravectors gives another paravector, but multiplying them generally produces a bivector term. Computations therefore take place in the full spatial Clifford algebra, even when the quantities being represented are paravectors.</p>\n</aside>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Encoding the algebra with Pauli matrices\n\nThe Pauli matrices are\n\n```math\n\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThey satisfy exactly the spatial Clifford relations:\n\n```math\n\\sigma_j\\sigma_k\n=\\delta_{jk}\\mathbf 1_2+i\\sum_{\\ell=1}^3\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}\\sigma_\\ell.\n```\n\nConsequently, the assignment ``e_j\\mapsto\\sigma_j`` extends to an algebra isomorphism\n\n```math\n\\rho:\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\longrightarrow M_2(\\mathbb C),\n\\qquad \\rho(AB)=\\rho(A)\\rho(B).\n```\n\nHere both sides are viewed as real algebras of dimension eight. Crucially, the geometric pseudoscalar is represented by the ordinary matrix imaginary unit:\n\n```math\n\\rho(I)=\\sigma_1\\sigma_2\\sigma_3=i\\mathbf 1_2.\n```\n\nFor example, ``e_1e_2=I e_3`` becomes ``\\sigma_1\\sigma_2=i\\sigma_3``. The matrices are therefore not merely a mnemonic for vector components: they represent the full geometric product.\n\nA paravector becomes the matrix\n\n```math\nP=\\rho(p)=p^0\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf p\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\n=\\begin{pmatrix}\np^0+p^3 & p^1-i p^2\\\\\np^1+i p^2 & p^0-p^3\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nFor real components, ``P`` is Hermitian: ``P^\\dagger=P``. Conversely, every Hermitian two-by-two matrix has exactly this form. Thus **real paravectors correspond precisely to Hermitian two-by-two matrices**.\n\nThe components are recovered without ambiguity by traces:\n\n```math\np^0=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}P,\n\\qquad\np^j=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}(P\\sigma_j).\n```\n\nFor arbitrary spatial vectors, the matrix counterpart of the geometric-product identity is\n\n```math\n(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)(\\mathbf b\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)\n=(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)\\mathbf 1_2\n+i(\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b)\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n```","output":"","result":"## Encoding the algebra with Pauli matrices\n\nThe Pauli matrices are\n\n```math\n\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThey satisfy exactly the spatial Clifford relations:\n\n```math\n\\sigma_j\\sigma_k\n=\\delta_{jk}\\mathbf 1_2+i\\sum_{\\ell=1}^3\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}\\sigma_\\ell.\n```\n\nConsequently, the assignment ``e_j\\mapsto\\sigma_j`` extends to an algebra isomorphism\n\n```math\n\\rho:\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\longrightarrow M_2(\\mathbb C),\n\\qquad \\rho(AB)=\\rho(A)\\rho(B).\n```\n\nHere both sides are viewed as real algebras of dimension eight. Crucially, the geometric pseudoscalar is represented by the ordinary matrix imaginary unit:\n\n```math\n\\rho(I)=\\sigma_1\\sigma_2\\sigma_3=i\\mathbf 1_2.\n```\n\nFor example, ``e_1e_2=I e_3`` becomes ``\\sigma_1\\sigma_2=i\\sigma_3``. The matrices are therefore not merely a mnemonic for vector components: they represent the full geometric product.\n\nA paravector becomes the matrix\n\n```math\nP=\\rho(p)=p^0\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf p\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\n=\\begin{pmatrix}\np^0+p^3 & p^1-i p^2\\\\\np^1+i p^2 & p^0-p^3\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nFor real components, ``P`` is Hermitian: ``P^\\dagger=P``. Conversely, every Hermitian two-by-two matrix has exactly this form. Thus **real paravectors correspond precisely to Hermitian two-by-two matrices**.\n\nThe components are recovered without ambiguity by traces:\n\n```math\np^0=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}P,\n\\qquad\np^j=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}(P\\sigma_j).\n```\n\nFor arbitrary spatial vectors, the matrix counterpart of the geometric-product identity is\n\n```math\n(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)(\\mathbf b\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)\n=(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)\\mathbf 1_2\n+i(\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b)\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n```","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4763-encoding-the-algebra-with-pauli-matrices\">\n<h1>Encoding the algebra with Pauli matrices</h1>\n<p>The Pauli matrices are</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-i\\\\i&amp;0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;-1\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>They satisfy exactly the spatial Clifford relations:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\sigma_j\\sigma_k\n=\\delta_{jk}\\mathbf 1_2+i\\sum_{\\ell=1}^3\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}\\sigma_\\ell.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Consequently, the assignment <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_j\\mapsto\\sigma_j\\)</span> extends to an algebra isomorphism</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho:\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\longrightarrow M_2(\\mathbb C),\n\\qquad \\rho(AB)=\\rho(A)\\rho(B).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Here both sides are viewed as real algebras of dimension eight. Crucially, the geometric pseudoscalar is represented by the ordinary matrix imaginary unit:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho(I)=\\sigma_1\\sigma_2\\sigma_3=i\\mathbf 1_2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For example, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_1e_2=I e_3\\)</span> becomes <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\sigma_1\\sigma_2=i\\sigma_3\\)</span>. The matrices are therefore not merely a mnemonic for vector components: they represent the full geometric product.</p>\n<p>A paravector becomes the matrix</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nP=\\rho(p)=p^0\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf p\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\n=\\begin{pmatrix}\np^0+p^3 &amp; p^1-i p^2\\\\\np^1+i p^2 &amp; p^0-p^3\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For real components, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P\\)</span> is Hermitian: <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P^\\dagger=P\\)</span>. Conversely, every Hermitian two-by-two matrix has exactly this form. Thus <strong>real paravectors correspond precisely to Hermitian two-by-two matrices</strong>.</p>\n<p>The components are recovered without ambiguity by traces:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np^0=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}P,\n\\qquad\np^j=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}(P\\sigma_j).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For arbitrary spatial vectors, the matrix counterpart of the geometric-product identity is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)(\\mathbf b\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)\n=(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)\\mathbf 1_2\n+i(\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b)\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n\\]\n</div>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## The natural complex basis\n\nBecause the pseudoscalar ``I`` is central and satisfies ``I^2=-1``, the scalar–pseudoscalar plane forms a copy of the complex numbers inside APS:\n\n```math\n\\mathbb C_I=\\{a+Ib:a,b\\in\\mathbb R\\}.\n```\n\nOver this central field, a natural basis for the entire algebra is\n\n```math\n\\{1,e_1,e_2,e_3\\}.\n```\n\nThus APS has dimension eight over the reals but dimension four over ``\\mathbb C_I``. This does **not** introduce an additional imaginary unit or enlarge the algebra to its complexification: the complex structure is already supplied by its real pseudoscalar.\n\n### From four real grades to a complex scalar and vector\n\nWrite a generic multivector, with all eight coefficients real, as\n\n```math\nA=a+v_1e_1+v_2e_2+v_3e_3\n+b_1e_2e_3+b_2e_3e_1+b_3e_1e_2+Ib.\n```\n\nUsing the duality identities for bivectors, collect the terms into\n\n```math\nA=(a+Ib)+\\sum_{j=1}^3(v_j+Ib_j)e_j\n=z+\\mathbf w,\n\\qquad\nz=a+Ib,\\qquad\n\\mathbf w=\\mathbf v+I\\mathbf b.\n```\n\nHere ``\\mathbf b=b_1e_1+b_2e_2+b_3e_3`` is the spatial dual of the bivector part: the bivector itself is ``I\\mathbf b``. The term **complex vector** means a linear combination of the spatial basis vectors with coefficients in ``\\mathbb C_I``; it need not be a pure grade-one element of the real Clifford algebra.\n\nThe grade content is therefore transparent:\n\n- The real and imaginary parts of ``z`` encode the scalar and pseudoscalar grades.\n- The real and imaginary parts of ``\\mathbf w`` encode the vector and bivector grades.\n- Real paravectors are the special case ``b=0`` and ``\\mathbf b=0``.\n\nFor example,\n\n```math\nA=2+3e_1-e_2+4e_2e_3-2e_1e_2+5I\n=(2+5I)+(3+4I)e_1-e_2-2Ie_3.\n```\n\n### Multiplication in complex scalar–vector form\n\nExtend the spatial dot and cross products complex-bilinearly. For ``A=z+\\mathbf w`` and ``C=s+\\mathbf u``, the geometric product becomes\n\n```math\nAC=(zs+\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf u)\n+\\left(z\\mathbf u+s\\mathbf w+I\\mathbf w\\times\\mathbf u\\right).\n```\n\nThere is **no complex conjugation** in this dot product: ``\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf u=\\sum_j w_j u_j``. It is not the positive Hermitian inner product used for complex state vectors.\n\n### Involutions and the general inverse\n\nClifford conjugation, denoted by an overbar, reverses products and sends each spatial basis vector to its negative. Reversion, denoted by a dagger, reverses products but fixes each spatial basis vector:\n\n```math\n\\overline{AC}=\\bar C\\bar A,\\qquad \\bar e_j=-e_j,\n\\qquad\n(AC)^\\dagger=C^\\dagger A^\\dagger,\\qquad e_j^\\dagger=e_j.\n```\n\nBoth operations are real-linear and fix real scalars. Their actions on the real grades are\n\n| Operation | Scalar | Vector | Bivector | Pseudoscalar |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Clifford conjugation | unchanged | sign reversed | sign reversed | unchanged |\n| Reversion | unchanged | unchanged | sign reversed | sign reversed |\n\nIn particular, Clifford conjugation fixes ``I``, whereas reversion sends it to ``-I``. Writing a star for coefficientwise conjugation in ``\\mathbb C_I``, their complex scalar–vector forms are\n\n```math\n\\bar A=z-\\mathbf w,\n\\qquad\nA^\\dagger=z^*+\\mathbf w^*.\n```\n\nClifford conjugation is complex-linear but reverses products; reversion is complex-antilinear and also reverses products. Neither operation means simply taking the real part. On matrices, reversion is represented by Hermitian conjugation:\n\n```math\n\\rho(A^\\dagger)=\\rho(A)^\\dagger.\n```\n\nThe product with the Clifford conjugate is central:\n\n```math\nA\\bar A=\\bar A A=z^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w\\in\\mathbb C_I.\n```\n\nConsequently, a generic element is invertible exactly when this complex scalar is nonzero, in which case\n\n```math\nA^{-1}=\\frac{\\bar A}{z^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w}.\n```\n\nFor real paravectors this specializes to the Minkowski-norm inverse already derived.\n\n### Connection to the Pauli encoding\n\nUnder the Pauli representation introduced above, ``I`` maps to the ordinary ``i``. If ``z=a+Ib`` maps to ``\\zeta=a+ib`` and each ``w_j=v_j+Ib_j`` maps to ``\\omega_j=v_j+ib_j``, then\n\n```math\n\\rho(A)=\\zeta\\mathbf 1_2+\\sum_{j=1}^3\\omega_j\\sigma_j\n=\\begin{pmatrix}\n\\zeta+\\omega_3&\\omega_1-i\\omega_2\\\\\n\\omega_1+i\\omega_2&\\zeta-\\omega_3\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThe identity and three Pauli matrices form a basis of all complex two-by-two matrices over the complex numbers. Their coefficients are\n\n```math\n\\zeta=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}\\rho(A),\n\\qquad\n\\omega_j=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(\\rho(A)\\sigma_j\\bigr).\n```\n\nFor the example above, this gives\n\n```math\n\\rho(A)=\\begin{pmatrix}2+3i&3+5i\\\\3+3i&2+7i\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nA general APS element therefore corresponds to an arbitrary complex matrix, whereas a real paravector corresponds to a Hermitian matrix. Clifford conjugation corresponds to the adjugate, not to entrywise complex conjugation:\n\n```math\n\\rho(\\bar A)=\\operatorname{adj}\\rho(A),\n\\qquad\n\\det\\rho(A)=\\zeta^2-\\sum_j\\omega_j^2.\n```\n\nFor a generic multivector this determinant can be complex. On the real paravector subspace, it is precisely the real Minkowski norm. Other multivectors can also have real determinants, so reality of the determinant alone does not characterize paravectors.","output":"","result":"## The natural complex basis\n\nBecause the pseudoscalar ``I`` is central and satisfies ``I^2=-1``, the scalar–pseudoscalar plane forms a copy of the complex numbers inside APS:\n\n```math\n\\mathbb C_I=\\{a+Ib:a,b\\in\\mathbb R\\}.\n```\n\nOver this central field, a natural basis for the entire algebra is\n\n```math\n\\{1,e_1,e_2,e_3\\}.\n```\n\nThus APS has dimension eight over the reals but dimension four over ``\\mathbb C_I``. This does **not** introduce an additional imaginary unit or enlarge the algebra to its complexification: the complex structure is already supplied by its real pseudoscalar.\n\n### From four real grades to a complex scalar and vector\n\nWrite a generic multivector, with all eight coefficients real, as\n\n```math\nA=a+v_1e_1+v_2e_2+v_3e_3\n+b_1e_2e_3+b_2e_3e_1+b_3e_1e_2+Ib.\n```\n\nUsing the duality identities for bivectors, collect the terms into\n\n```math\nA=(a+Ib)+\\sum_{j=1}^3(v_j+Ib_j)e_j\n=z+\\mathbf w,\n\\qquad\nz=a+Ib,\\qquad\n\\mathbf w=\\mathbf v+I\\mathbf b.\n```\n\nHere ``\\mathbf b=b_1e_1+b_2e_2+b_3e_3`` is the spatial dual of the bivector part: the bivector itself is ``I\\mathbf b``. The term **complex vector** means a linear combination of the spatial basis vectors with coefficients in ``\\mathbb C_I``; it need not be a pure grade-one element of the real Clifford algebra.\n\nThe grade content is therefore transparent:\n\n- The real and imaginary parts of ``z`` encode the scalar and pseudoscalar grades.\n- The real and imaginary parts of ``\\mathbf w`` encode the vector and bivector grades.\n- Real paravectors are the special case ``b=0`` and ``\\mathbf b=0``.\n\nFor example,\n\n```math\nA=2+3e_1-e_2+4e_2e_3-2e_1e_2+5I\n=(2+5I)+(3+4I)e_1-e_2-2Ie_3.\n```\n\n### Multiplication in complex scalar–vector form\n\nExtend the spatial dot and cross products complex-bilinearly. For ``A=z+\\mathbf w`` and ``C=s+\\mathbf u``, the geometric product becomes\n\n```math\nAC=(zs+\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf u)\n+\\left(z\\mathbf u+s\\mathbf w+I\\mathbf w\\times\\mathbf u\\right).\n```\n\nThere is **no complex conjugation** in this dot product: ``\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf u=\\sum_j w_j u_j``. It is not the positive Hermitian inner product used for complex state vectors.\n\n### Involutions and the general inverse\n\nClifford conjugation, denoted by an overbar, reverses products and sends each spatial basis vector to its negative. Reversion, denoted by a dagger, reverses products but fixes each spatial basis vector:\n\n```math\n\\overline{AC}=\\bar C\\bar A,\\qquad \\bar e_j=-e_j,\n\\qquad\n(AC)^\\dagger=C^\\dagger A^\\dagger,\\qquad e_j^\\dagger=e_j.\n```\n\nBoth operations are real-linear and fix real scalars. Their actions on the real grades are\n\n| Operation | Scalar | Vector | Bivector | Pseudoscalar |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Clifford conjugation | unchanged | sign reversed | sign reversed | unchanged |\n| Reversion | unchanged | unchanged | sign reversed | sign reversed |\n\nIn particular, Clifford conjugation fixes ``I``, whereas reversion sends it to ``-I``. Writing a star for coefficientwise conjugation in ``\\mathbb C_I``, their complex scalar–vector forms are\n\n```math\n\\bar A=z-\\mathbf w,\n\\qquad\nA^\\dagger=z^*+\\mathbf w^*.\n```\n\nClifford conjugation is complex-linear but reverses products; reversion is complex-antilinear and also reverses products. Neither operation means simply taking the real part. On matrices, reversion is represented by Hermitian conjugation:\n\n```math\n\\rho(A^\\dagger)=\\rho(A)^\\dagger.\n```\n\nThe product with the Clifford conjugate is central:\n\n```math\nA\\bar A=\\bar A A=z^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w\\in\\mathbb C_I.\n```\n\nConsequently, a generic element is invertible exactly when this complex scalar is nonzero, in which case\n\n```math\nA^{-1}=\\frac{\\bar A}{z^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w}.\n```\n\nFor real paravectors this specializes to the Minkowski-norm inverse already derived.\n\n### Connection to the Pauli encoding\n\nUnder the Pauli representation introduced above, ``I`` maps to the ordinary ``i``. If ``z=a+Ib`` maps to ``\\zeta=a+ib`` and each ``w_j=v_j+Ib_j`` maps to ``\\omega_j=v_j+ib_j``, then\n\n```math\n\\rho(A)=\\zeta\\mathbf 1_2+\\sum_{j=1}^3\\omega_j\\sigma_j\n=\\begin{pmatrix}\n\\zeta+\\omega_3&\\omega_1-i\\omega_2\\\\\n\\omega_1+i\\omega_2&\\zeta-\\omega_3\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThe identity and three Pauli matrices form a basis of all complex two-by-two matrices over the complex numbers. Their coefficients are\n\n```math\n\\zeta=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}\\rho(A),\n\\qquad\n\\omega_j=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(\\rho(A)\\sigma_j\\bigr).\n```\n\nFor the example above, this gives\n\n```math\n\\rho(A)=\\begin{pmatrix}2+3i&3+5i\\\\3+3i&2+7i\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nA general APS element therefore corresponds to an arbitrary complex matrix, whereas a real paravector corresponds to a Hermitian matrix. Clifford conjugation corresponds to the adjugate, not to entrywise complex conjugation:\n\n```math\n\\rho(\\bar A)=\\operatorname{adj}\\rho(A),\n\\qquad\n\\det\\rho(A)=\\zeta^2-\\sum_j\\omega_j^2.\n```\n\nFor a generic multivector this determinant can be complex. On the real paravector subspace, it is precisely the real Minkowski norm. Other multivectors can also have real determinants, so reality of the determinant alone does not characterize paravectors.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4766-the-natural-complex-basis\">\n<h1>The natural complex basis</h1>\n<p>Because the pseudoscalar <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I\\)</span> is central and satisfies <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I^2=-1\\)</span>, the scalar–pseudoscalar plane forms a copy of the complex numbers inside APS:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathbb C_I=\\{a+Ib:a,b\\in\\mathbb R\\}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Over this central field, a natural basis for the entire algebra is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\{1,e_1,e_2,e_3\\}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Thus APS has dimension eight over the reals but dimension four over <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbb C_I\\)</span>. This does <strong>not</strong> introduce an additional imaginary unit or enlarge the algebra to its complexification: the complex structure is already supplied by its real pseudoscalar.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4766-from-four-real-grades-to-a-complex-scalar-and-vector\">\n<h1>From four real grades to a complex scalar and vector</h1>\n<p>Write a generic multivector, with all eight coefficients real, as</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA=a+v_1e_1+v_2e_2+v_3e_3\n+b_1e_2e_3+b_2e_3e_1+b_3e_1e_2+Ib.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Using the duality identities for bivectors, collect the terms into</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA=(a+Ib)+\\sum_{j=1}^3(v_j+Ib_j)e_j\n=z+\\mathbf w,\n\\qquad\nz=a+Ib,\\qquad\n\\mathbf w=\\mathbf v+I\\mathbf b.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Here <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf b=b_1e_1+b_2e_2+b_3e_3\\)</span> is the spatial dual of the bivector part: the bivector itself is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I\\mathbf b\\)</span>. The term <strong>complex vector</strong> means a linear combination of the spatial basis vectors with coefficients in <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbb C_I\\)</span>; it need not be a pure grade-one element of the real Clifford algebra.</p>\n<p>The grade content is therefore transparent:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The real and imaginary parts of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(z\\)</span> encode the scalar and pseudoscalar grades.</li>\n<li>The real and imaginary parts of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf w\\)</span> encode the vector and bivector grades.</li>\n<li>Real paravectors are the special case <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(b=0\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf b=0\\)</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For example,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA=2+3e_1-e_2+4e_2e_3-2e_1e_2+5I\n=(2+5I)+(3+4I)e_1-e_2-2Ie_3.\n\\]\n</div>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4766-multiplication-in-complex-scalarvector-form\">\n<h1>Multiplication in complex scalar–vector form</h1>\n<p>Extend the spatial dot and cross products complex-bilinearly. For <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A=z+\\mathbf w\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(C=s+\\mathbf u\\)</span>, the geometric product becomes</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nAC=(zs+\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf u)\n+\\left(z\\mathbf u+s\\mathbf w+I\\mathbf w\\times\\mathbf u\\right).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>There is <strong>no complex conjugation</strong> in this dot product: <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf u=\\sum_j w_j u_j\\)</span>. It is not the positive Hermitian inner product used for complex state vectors.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4766-involutions-and-the-general-inverse\">\n<h1>Involutions and the general inverse</h1>\n<p>Clifford conjugation, denoted by an overbar, reverses products and sends each spatial basis vector to its negative. Reversion, denoted by a dagger, reverses products but fixes each spatial basis vector:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\overline{AC}=\\bar C\\bar A,\\qquad \\bar e_j=-e_j,\n\\qquad\n(AC)^\\dagger=C^\\dagger A^\\dagger,\\qquad e_j^\\dagger=e_j.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Both operations are real-linear and fix real scalars. Their actions on the real grades are</p>\n<figure data-type=\"table\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Operation</th>\n<th>Scalar</th>\n<th>Vector</th>\n<th>Bivector</th>\n<th>Pseudoscalar</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clifford conjugation</td>\n<td>unchanged</td>\n<td>sign reversed</td>\n<td>sign reversed</td>\n<td>unchanged</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reversion</td>\n<td>unchanged</td>\n<td>unchanged</td>\n<td>sign reversed</td>\n<td>sign reversed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</figure>\n<p>In particular, Clifford conjugation fixes <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I\\)</span>, whereas reversion sends it to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-I\\)</span>. Writing a star for coefficientwise conjugation in <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbb C_I\\)</span>, their complex scalar–vector forms are</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\bar A=z-\\mathbf w,\n\\qquad\nA^\\dagger=z^*+\\mathbf w^*.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Clifford conjugation is complex-linear but reverses products; reversion is complex-antilinear and also reverses products. Neither operation means simply taking the real part. On matrices, reversion is represented by Hermitian conjugation:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho(A^\\dagger)=\\rho(A)^\\dagger.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The product with the Clifford conjugate is central:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA\\bar A=\\bar A A=z^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w\\in\\mathbb C_I.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Consequently, a generic element is invertible exactly when this complex scalar is nonzero, in which case</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA^{-1}=\\frac{\\bar A}{z^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For real paravectors this specializes to the Minkowski-norm inverse already derived.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4766-connection-to-the-pauli-encoding\">\n<h1>Connection to the Pauli encoding</h1>\n<p>Under the Pauli representation introduced above, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I\\)</span> maps to the ordinary <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(i\\)</span>. If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(z=a+Ib\\)</span> maps to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\zeta=a+ib\\)</span> and each <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(w_j=v_j+Ib_j\\)</span> maps to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\omega_j=v_j+ib_j\\)</span>, then</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho(A)=\\zeta\\mathbf 1_2+\\sum_{j=1}^3\\omega_j\\sigma_j\n=\\begin{pmatrix}\n\\zeta+\\omega_3&amp;\\omega_1-i\\omega_2\\\\\n\\omega_1+i\\omega_2&amp;\\zeta-\\omega_3\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The identity and three Pauli matrices form a basis of all complex two-by-two matrices over the complex numbers. Their coefficients are</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\zeta=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}\\rho(A),\n\\qquad\n\\omega_j=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(\\rho(A)\\sigma_j\\bigr).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For the example above, this gives</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho(A)=\\begin{pmatrix}2+3i&amp;3+5i\\\\3+3i&amp;2+7i\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>A general APS element therefore corresponds to an arbitrary complex matrix, whereas a real paravector corresponds to a Hermitian matrix. Clifford conjugation corresponds to the adjugate, not to entrywise complex conjugation:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho(\\bar A)=\\operatorname{adj}\\rho(A),\n\\qquad\n\\det\\rho(A)=\\zeta^2-\\sum_j\\omega_j^2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For a generic multivector this determinant can be complex. On the real paravector subspace, it is precisely the real Minkowski norm. Other multivectors can also have real determinants, so reality of the determinant alone does not characterize paravectors.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Grade projections and the even subalgebra\n\n### Grade involution and projection formulas\n\nIn addition to reversion and Clifford conjugation, the **grade involution** changes the sign of odd grades:\n\n```math\n\\widehat{A_r}=(-1)^r A_r,\n\\qquad\n\\widehat{AC}=\\widehat A\\widehat C,\n\\qquad\n\\bar A=\\widehat{A^\\dagger}.\n```\n\nIt is an automorphism, whereas reversion and Clifford conjugation reverse product order. The three operations commute and square to the identity. If\n\n```math\nA=a+\\mathbf v+I\\mathbf b+Ib,\n```\n\nthen their simultaneous sign patterns recover each real grade:\n\n```math\n\\langle A\\rangle_0=\\frac14(A+\\widehat A+A^\\dagger+\\bar A),\n\\qquad\n\\langle A\\rangle_1=\\frac14(A-\\widehat A+A^\\dagger-\\bar A),\n```\n\n```math\n\\langle A\\rangle_2=\\frac14(A+\\widehat A-A^\\dagger-\\bar A),\n\\qquad\n\\langle A\\rangle_3=\\frac14(A-\\widehat A-A^\\dagger+\\bar A).\n```\n\nThese are real-linear projections. They must not be confused with projecting onto the complex scalar and complex vector, which instead gives\n\n```math\nz=\\frac12(A+\\bar A)=a+Ib,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf w=\\frac12(A-\\bar A)=\\mathbf v+I\\mathbf b.\n```\n\nGrade involution sends ``I`` to ``-I``. In complex scalar–vector notation it therefore acts as ``\\widehat A=z^*-\\mathbf w^*``, with the star conjugating coefficients. It is not linear over the central complex field.\n\n### Quaternions inside APS\n\nThe even elements, consisting of scalar and bivector parts, form a real subalgebra:\n\n```math\n\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}^{+}=\\{a+I\\mathbf b:a\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\mathbf b\\in\\mathbb R^3\\}.\n```\n\nChoose the quaternion units with the signs\n\n```math\nq_1=-Ie_1=-e_2e_3,\\qquad\nq_2=-Ie_2=-e_3e_1,\\qquad\nq_3=-Ie_3=-e_1e_2.\n```\n\nDirect multiplication gives\n\n```math\nq_jq_k=-\\delta_{jk}+\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}q_\\ell,\n\\qquad\nq_1q_2=q_3,\\qquad q_1q_2q_3=-1.\n```\n\nThus ``1,q_1,q_2,q_3`` realize the usual quaternion basis. Taking all three units as ``+Ie_j`` would reverse the sign of the cross term and would not give this multiplication convention.\n\nFor ``Q=a+\\sum_j c_jq_j``, quaternion conjugation agrees with reversion restricted to the even subalgebra:\n\n```math\nQ^\\dagger=a-\\sum_jc_jq_j,\n\\qquad\nQQ^\\dagger=a^2+\\sum_jc_j^2.\n```\n\nEvery nonzero even element is therefore invertible. This contrasts with the full APS algebra, which has zero divisors. Also, the even subalgebra is not a complex subspace over ``\\mathbb C_I``: multiplication by ``I`` changes even grades into odd ones.\n\n### Unit quaternions and rotations\n\nThe unit even elements form the group ``\\mathrm{Spin}(3)\\cong\\mathrm{SU}(2)``. A rotation about the unit vector ``\\mathbf n`` has representative\n\n```math\nR=\\cos\\frac\\theta2-I\\mathbf n\\sin\\frac\\theta2,\n\\qquad RR^\\dagger=1.\n```\n\nExpanding ``\\mathbf v'=R\\mathbf vR^\\dagger`` yields Rodrigues' formula:\n\n```math\n\\mathbf v'=\\mathbf v\\cos\\theta\n+(\\mathbf n\\times\\mathbf v)\\sin\\theta\n+\\mathbf n(\\mathbf n\\cdot\\mathbf v)(1-\\cos\\theta).\n```\n\nBoth ``R`` and ``-R`` give the same rotation. The kernel of this action is exactly ``\\{1,-1\\}``, giving the double covering of ``\\mathrm{SO}(3)``. For example, ``R=(1-Ie_3)/\\sqrt2`` sends ``e_1`` to ``e_2``.\n\n### Exercises on projections and quaternions\n\n1. Apply the four grade projections to ``2+3e_1-e_2+4e_2e_3-2e_1e_2+5I``.\n2. Verify ``q_2q_1=-q_3`` and derive the inverse of an arbitrary nonzero quaternion using reversion.\n3. Derive Rodrigues' formula and check the axis component is unchanged.","output":"","result":"## Grade projections and the even subalgebra\n\n### Grade involution and projection formulas\n\nIn addition to reversion and Clifford conjugation, the **grade involution** changes the sign of odd grades:\n\n```math\n\\widehat{A_r}=(-1)^r A_r,\n\\qquad\n\\widehat{AC}=\\widehat A\\widehat C,\n\\qquad\n\\bar A=\\widehat{A^\\dagger}.\n```\n\nIt is an automorphism, whereas reversion and Clifford conjugation reverse product order. The three operations commute and square to the identity. If\n\n```math\nA=a+\\mathbf v+I\\mathbf b+Ib,\n```\n\nthen their simultaneous sign patterns recover each real grade:\n\n```math\n\\langle A\\rangle_0=\\frac14(A+\\widehat A+A^\\dagger+\\bar A),\n\\qquad\n\\langle A\\rangle_1=\\frac14(A-\\widehat A+A^\\dagger-\\bar A),\n```\n\n```math\n\\langle A\\rangle_2=\\frac14(A+\\widehat A-A^\\dagger-\\bar A),\n\\qquad\n\\langle A\\rangle_3=\\frac14(A-\\widehat A-A^\\dagger+\\bar A).\n```\n\nThese are real-linear projections. They must not be confused with projecting onto the complex scalar and complex vector, which instead gives\n\n```math\nz=\\frac12(A+\\bar A)=a+Ib,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf w=\\frac12(A-\\bar A)=\\mathbf v+I\\mathbf b.\n```\n\nGrade involution sends ``I`` to ``-I``. In complex scalar–vector notation it therefore acts as ``\\widehat A=z^*-\\mathbf w^*``, with the star conjugating coefficients. It is not linear over the central complex field.\n\n### Quaternions inside APS\n\nThe even elements, consisting of scalar and bivector parts, form a real subalgebra:\n\n```math\n\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}^{+}=\\{a+I\\mathbf b:a\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\mathbf b\\in\\mathbb R^3\\}.\n```\n\nChoose the quaternion units with the signs\n\n```math\nq_1=-Ie_1=-e_2e_3,\\qquad\nq_2=-Ie_2=-e_3e_1,\\qquad\nq_3=-Ie_3=-e_1e_2.\n```\n\nDirect multiplication gives\n\n```math\nq_jq_k=-\\delta_{jk}+\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}q_\\ell,\n\\qquad\nq_1q_2=q_3,\\qquad q_1q_2q_3=-1.\n```\n\nThus ``1,q_1,q_2,q_3`` realize the usual quaternion basis. Taking all three units as ``+Ie_j`` would reverse the sign of the cross term and would not give this multiplication convention.\n\nFor ``Q=a+\\sum_j c_jq_j``, quaternion conjugation agrees with reversion restricted to the even subalgebra:\n\n```math\nQ^\\dagger=a-\\sum_jc_jq_j,\n\\qquad\nQQ^\\dagger=a^2+\\sum_jc_j^2.\n```\n\nEvery nonzero even element is therefore invertible. This contrasts with the full APS algebra, which has zero divisors. Also, the even subalgebra is not a complex subspace over ``\\mathbb C_I``: multiplication by ``I`` changes even grades into odd ones.\n\n### Unit quaternions and rotations\n\nThe unit even elements form the group ``\\mathrm{Spin}(3)\\cong\\mathrm{SU}(2)``. A rotation about the unit vector ``\\mathbf n`` has representative\n\n```math\nR=\\cos\\frac\\theta2-I\\mathbf n\\sin\\frac\\theta2,\n\\qquad RR^\\dagger=1.\n```\n\nExpanding ``\\mathbf v'=R\\mathbf vR^\\dagger`` yields Rodrigues' formula:\n\n```math\n\\mathbf v'=\\mathbf v\\cos\\theta\n+(\\mathbf n\\times\\mathbf v)\\sin\\theta\n+\\mathbf n(\\mathbf n\\cdot\\mathbf v)(1-\\cos\\theta).\n```\n\nBoth ``R`` and ``-R`` give the same rotation. The kernel of this action is exactly ``\\{1,-1\\}``, giving the double covering of ``\\mathrm{SO}(3)``. For example, ``R=(1-Ie_3)/\\sqrt2`` sends ``e_1`` to ``e_2``.\n\n### Exercises on projections and quaternions\n\n1. Apply the four grade projections to ``2+3e_1-e_2+4e_2e_3-2e_1e_2+5I``.\n2. Verify ``q_2q_1=-q_3`` and derive the inverse of an arbitrary nonzero quaternion using reversion.\n3. Derive Rodrigues' formula and check the axis component is unchanged.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4769-grade-projections-and-the-even-subalgebra\">\n<h1>Grade projections and the even subalgebra</h1>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4769-grade-involution-and-projection-formulas\">\n<h1>Grade involution and projection formulas</h1>\n<p>In addition to reversion and Clifford conjugation, the <strong>grade involution</strong> changes the sign of odd grades:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\widehat{A_r}=(-1)^r A_r,\n\\qquad\n\\widehat{AC}=\\widehat A\\widehat C,\n\\qquad\n\\bar A=\\widehat{A^\\dagger}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>It is an automorphism, whereas reversion and Clifford conjugation reverse product order. The three operations commute and square to the identity. If</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA=a+\\mathbf v+I\\mathbf b+Ib,\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>then their simultaneous sign patterns recover each real grade:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\langle A\\rangle_0=\\frac14(A+\\widehat A+A^\\dagger+\\bar A),\n\\qquad\n\\langle A\\rangle_1=\\frac14(A-\\widehat A+A^\\dagger-\\bar A),\n\\]\n</div>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\langle A\\rangle_2=\\frac14(A+\\widehat A-A^\\dagger-\\bar A),\n\\qquad\n\\langle A\\rangle_3=\\frac14(A-\\widehat A-A^\\dagger+\\bar A).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>These are real-linear projections. They must not be confused with projecting onto the complex scalar and complex vector, which instead gives</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nz=\\frac12(A+\\bar A)=a+Ib,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf w=\\frac12(A-\\bar A)=\\mathbf v+I\\mathbf b.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Grade involution sends <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I\\)</span> to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-I\\)</span>. In complex scalar–vector notation it therefore acts as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\widehat A=z^*-\\mathbf w^*\\)</span>, with the star conjugating coefficients. It is not linear over the central complex field.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4769-quaternions-inside-aps\">\n<h1>Quaternions inside APS</h1>\n<p>The even elements, consisting of scalar and bivector parts, form a real subalgebra:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}^{+}=\\{a+I\\mathbf b:a\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\mathbf b\\in\\mathbb R^3\\}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Choose the quaternion units with the signs</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nq_1=-Ie_1=-e_2e_3,\\qquad\nq_2=-Ie_2=-e_3e_1,\\qquad\nq_3=-Ie_3=-e_1e_2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Direct multiplication gives</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nq_jq_k=-\\delta_{jk}+\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}q_\\ell,\n\\qquad\nq_1q_2=q_3,\\qquad q_1q_2q_3=-1.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Thus <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1,q_1,q_2,q_3\\)</span> realize the usual quaternion basis. Taking all three units as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(+Ie_j\\)</span> would reverse the sign of the cross term and would not give this multiplication convention.</p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(Q=a+\\sum_j c_jq_j\\)</span>, quaternion conjugation agrees with reversion restricted to the even subalgebra:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nQ^\\dagger=a-\\sum_jc_jq_j,\n\\qquad\nQQ^\\dagger=a^2+\\sum_jc_j^2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Every nonzero even element is therefore invertible. This contrasts with the full APS algebra, which has zero divisors. Also, the even subalgebra is not a complex subspace over <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbb C_I\\)</span>: multiplication by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I\\)</span> changes even grades into odd ones.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4769-unit-quaternions-and-rotations\">\n<h1>Unit quaternions and rotations</h1>\n<p>The unit even elements form the group <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{Spin}(3)\\cong\\mathrm{SU}(2)\\)</span>. A rotation about the unit vector <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf n\\)</span> has representative</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nR=\\cos\\frac\\theta2-I\\mathbf n\\sin\\frac\\theta2,\n\\qquad RR^\\dagger=1.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Expanding <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf v'=R\\mathbf vR^\\dagger\\)</span> yields Rodrigues’ formula:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathbf v'=\\mathbf v\\cos\\theta\n+(\\mathbf n\\times\\mathbf v)\\sin\\theta\n+\\mathbf n(\\mathbf n\\cdot\\mathbf v)(1-\\cos\\theta).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Both <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(R\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-R\\)</span> give the same rotation. The kernel of this action is exactly <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\{1,-1\\}\\)</span>, giving the double covering of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{SO}(3)\\)</span>. For example, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(R=(1-Ie_3)/\\sqrt2\\)</span> sends <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_1\\)</span> to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_2\\)</span>.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4769-exercises-on-projections-and-quaternions\">\n<h1>Exercises on projections and quaternions</h1>\n<ol>\n<li>Apply the four grade projections to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(2+3e_1-e_2+4e_2e_3-2e_1e_2+5I\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>Verify <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(q_2q_1=-q_3\\)</span> and derive the inverse of an arbitrary nonzero quaternion using reversion.</li>\n<li>Derive Rodrigues’ formula and check the axis component is unchanged.</li>\n</ol>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Determinants, conjugation, and a worked example\n\nThe Pauli encoding turns the Minkowski norm into a determinant:\n\n```math\n\\det P=(p^0+p^3)(p^0-p^3)-(p^1-i p^2)(p^1+i p^2)\n=(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2.\n```\n\nThe conjugate paravector is represented by the adjugate matrix:\n\n```math\n\\rho(\\bar p)=p^0\\mathbf 1_2-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\n=\\operatorname{adj}P,\n\\qquad\nP\\operatorname{adj}P=(\\det P)\\mathbf 1_2.\n```\n\n**Do not confuse Clifford conjugation with Hermitian conjugation.** Hermitian conjugation leaves a real paravector matrix unchanged, whereas Clifford conjugation reverses its spatial part. More generally, reversion in APS reverses the order of geometric factors and is represented by Hermitian conjugation. Clifford conjugation is a different anti-involution.\n\nConsider the dimensionless example\n\n```math\np=5+e_1+2e_2+3e_3,\n\\qquad\nP=\\begin{pmatrix}8&1-2i\\\\1+2i&2\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThen\n\n```math\np\\bar p=25-1-4-9=11,\n\\qquad\n\\det P=16-(1-2i)(1+2i)=11.\n```\n\nBoth descriptions give the same inverse:\n\n```math\np^{-1}=\\frac{5-e_1-2e_2-3e_3}{11},\n\\qquad\nP^{-1}=\\frac1{11}\\begin{pmatrix}2&-1+2i\\\\-1-2i&8\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThe eigenvalues of any real paravector matrix are\n\n```math\n\\lambda_\\pm=p^0\\pm|\\mathbf p|.\n```\n\nHence future-directed timelike paravectors correspond to positive-definite Hermitian matrices. Future-directed nonzero null paravectors correspond to rank-one positive-semidefinite matrices. For instance,\n\n```math\nk=1+e_3\n\\quad\\longleftrightarrow\\quad\nK=\\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad \\det K=0.\n```\n\nSuch a matrix can be factored as ``K=\\psi\\psi^\\dagger``, here with ``\\psi=(\\sqrt{2},0)^T``. This is one entry point to the relation between null directions and two-component spinors.","output":"","result":"## Determinants, conjugation, and a worked example\n\nThe Pauli encoding turns the Minkowski norm into a determinant:\n\n```math\n\\det P=(p^0+p^3)(p^0-p^3)-(p^1-i p^2)(p^1+i p^2)\n=(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2.\n```\n\nThe conjugate paravector is represented by the adjugate matrix:\n\n```math\n\\rho(\\bar p)=p^0\\mathbf 1_2-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\n=\\operatorname{adj}P,\n\\qquad\nP\\operatorname{adj}P=(\\det P)\\mathbf 1_2.\n```\n\n**Do not confuse Clifford conjugation with Hermitian conjugation.** Hermitian conjugation leaves a real paravector matrix unchanged, whereas Clifford conjugation reverses its spatial part. More generally, reversion in APS reverses the order of geometric factors and is represented by Hermitian conjugation. Clifford conjugation is a different anti-involution.\n\nConsider the dimensionless example\n\n```math\np=5+e_1+2e_2+3e_3,\n\\qquad\nP=\\begin{pmatrix}8&1-2i\\\\1+2i&2\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThen\n\n```math\np\\bar p=25-1-4-9=11,\n\\qquad\n\\det P=16-(1-2i)(1+2i)=11.\n```\n\nBoth descriptions give the same inverse:\n\n```math\np^{-1}=\\frac{5-e_1-2e_2-3e_3}{11},\n\\qquad\nP^{-1}=\\frac1{11}\\begin{pmatrix}2&-1+2i\\\\-1-2i&8\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThe eigenvalues of any real paravector matrix are\n\n```math\n\\lambda_\\pm=p^0\\pm|\\mathbf p|.\n```\n\nHence future-directed timelike paravectors correspond to positive-definite Hermitian matrices. Future-directed nonzero null paravectors correspond to rank-one positive-semidefinite matrices. For instance,\n\n```math\nk=1+e_3\n\\quad\\longleftrightarrow\\quad\nK=\\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad \\det K=0.\n```\n\nSuch a matrix can be factored as ``K=\\psi\\psi^\\dagger``, here with ``\\psi=(\\sqrt{2},0)^T``. This is one entry point to the relation between null directions and two-component spinors.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4762-determinants-conjugation-and-a-worked-example\">\n<h1>Determinants, conjugation, and a worked example</h1>\n<p>The Pauli encoding turns the Minkowski norm into a determinant:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\det P=(p^0+p^3)(p^0-p^3)-(p^1-i p^2)(p^1+i p^2)\n=(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The conjugate paravector is represented by the adjugate matrix:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho(\\bar p)=p^0\\mathbf 1_2-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\n=\\operatorname{adj}P,\n\\qquad\nP\\operatorname{adj}P=(\\det P)\\mathbf 1_2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p><strong>Do not confuse Clifford conjugation with Hermitian conjugation.</strong> Hermitian conjugation leaves a real paravector matrix unchanged, whereas Clifford conjugation reverses its spatial part. More generally, reversion in APS reverses the order of geometric factors and is represented by Hermitian conjugation. Clifford conjugation is a different anti-involution.</p>\n<p>Consider the dimensionless example</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np=5+e_1+2e_2+3e_3,\n\\qquad\nP=\\begin{pmatrix}8&amp;1-2i\\\\1+2i&amp;2\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Then</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np\\bar p=25-1-4-9=11,\n\\qquad\n\\det P=16-(1-2i)(1+2i)=11.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Both descriptions give the same inverse:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np^{-1}=\\frac{5-e_1-2e_2-3e_3}{11},\n\\qquad\nP^{-1}=\\frac1{11}\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;-1+2i\\\\-1-2i&amp;8\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The eigenvalues of any real paravector matrix are</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lambda_\\pm=p^0\\pm|\\mathbf p|.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Hence future-directed timelike paravectors correspond to positive-definite Hermitian matrices. Future-directed nonzero null paravectors correspond to rank-one positive-semidefinite matrices. For instance,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nk=1+e_3\n\\quad\\longleftrightarrow\\quad\nK=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;0\\\\0&amp;0\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad \\det K=0.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Such a matrix can be factored as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(K=\\psi\\psi^\\dagger\\)</span>, here with <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\psi=(\\sqrt{2},0)^T\\)</span>. This is one entry point to the relation between null directions and two-component spinors.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Zero divisors, idempotents, and spectral projectors\n\nA nonzero element ``A`` is a **zero divisor** if it annihilates some nonzero element on at least one side. Since APS is isomorphic to complex two-by-two matrices, its nonzero noninvertible elements have rank one and are zero divisors. Equivalently, for ``A=z+\\mathbf w``,\n\n```math\nz^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w=0.\n```\n\nThe word *null* has referred to real paravectors and their Minkowski norm; a generic singular multivector need not be a real paravector.\n\n### Idempotents and complementary ideals\n\nAn **idempotent** satisfies ``f^2=f``. If ``\\mathbf n`` is a real unit spatial vector, define\n\n```math\nf_\\pm=\\frac{1\\pm\\mathbf n}{2}.\n```\n\nThen\n\n```math\nf_\\pm^2=f_\\pm,\\qquad f_+f_-=f_-f_+=0,\n\\qquad f_++f_-=1,\\qquad f_\\pm^\\dagger=f_\\pm.\n```\n\nTheir matrices are orthogonal rank-one projectors. These idempotents are **primitive**: they cannot be decomposed into a sum of two nonzero mutually annihilating idempotents. Every nontrivial idempotent in complex two-by-two matrices has rank one and is primitive, although it need not be Hermitian.\n\nWriting ``\\mathcal A=\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}``, right multiplication by ``f_\\pm`` splits the algebra into left ideals:\n\n```math\n\\mathcal A=\\mathcal A f_+\\oplus\\mathcal A f_-.\n```\n\nEach ideal is stable under left multiplication by arbitrary algebra elements. For ``\\mathbf n=e_3``, the two matrix ideals consist of matrices with only the first or only the second column nonzero. Each has complex dimension two and is a spinor module, not a subalgebra with the original identity.\n\nTo see minimality, take any nonzero first column ``v``. By choosing a complex linear map ``M`` with ``Mv=u``, left multiplication can turn it into any prescribed column ``u``. Thus a nonzero left ideal contained in the first-column ideal must contain the entire ideal. This supplies the algebraic justification for the spinor construction later in the chapter.\n\n### Spectral decomposition of real paravectors\n\nFor ``p=p^0+\\mathbf p`` with ``r=|\\mathbf p|>0``, set ``\\mathbf n=\\mathbf p/r``. Then\n\n```math\np=(p^0+r)f_++(p^0-r)f_-.\n```\n\nUnder the Pauli map this is precisely the Hermitian spectral theorem. The projectors are orthogonal, and the eigenvalues are real. For example,\n\n```math\np=3+2e_1,\n\\qquad\nf_\\pm=\\frac{1\\pm e_1}{2},\n\\qquad p=5f_++f_-.\n```\n\nWhen ``\\mathbf p=0``, the matrix is scalar: both eigenvalues coincide and no direction is selected. If both eigenvalues are positive, choose orthonormal eigenvectors ``u_\\pm`` and obtain\n\n```math\nP=\\psi_+\\psi_+^\\dagger+\\psi_-\\psi_-^\\dagger,\n\\qquad\n\\psi_\\pm=\\sqrt{p^0\\pm r}\\,u_\\pm.\n```\n\nThis proves the two-spinor decomposition of a future timelike paravector matrix.\n\n### Optional extension: general complex spectra and Jordan form\n\nFor a generic element ``A=z+\\mathbf w``, choose ``\\lambda\\in\\mathbb C_I`` with ``\\lambda^2=\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w``. If ``\\lambda\\ne0``, define\n\n```math\nF_\\pm=\\frac12\\left(1\\pm\\frac{\\mathbf w}{\\lambda}\\right).\n```\n\nThe identity ``\\mathbf w^2=\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w`` gives\n\n```math\nF_\\pm^2=F_\\pm,\\qquad F_+F_-=0,\n\\qquad A=(z+\\lambda)F_++(z-\\lambda)F_-.\n```\n\nThe projectors need not be Hermitian. Changing the square-root branch merely swaps their labels. The characteristic identity is\n\n```math\nA^2-2zA+(z^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w)1=0.\n```\n\nIf ``\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w=0`` but ``\\mathbf w\\ne0``, then ``\\mathbf w^2=0``: it is a nonzero nilpotent. The element ``A=z+\\mathbf w`` has a repeated eigenvalue and is not diagonalizable. An explicit example is\n\n```math\nN=e_1+Ie_2,\n\\qquad N^2=0,\n\\qquad \\rho(N)=\\begin{pmatrix}0&2\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nBy contrast, if ``\\mathbf w=0``, the element is scalar and diagonalizable. These are all the repeated-eigenvalue cases for two-by-two matrices.\n\nBeing singular, nilpotent, and idempotent are different properties. The singular paravector ``1+e_3`` has square ``2(1+e_3)`` and is neither idempotent nor nilpotent; its half is idempotent. Also ``1+N`` is invertible despite not being diagonalizable, with inverse ``1-N``.\n\n### Exercises on singular elements and spectra\n\n1. Verify ``(1+e_3)(1-e_3)=0`` and explain why neither factor can be inverted.\n2. Find the spectral projectors of ``3+e_1+2e_2+2e_3``. Check that its eigenvalues are ``6`` and ``0``.\n3. Show that ``(1+N)^{-1}=1-N`` and determine its determinant and repeated eigenvalue.\n4. Construct a non-Hermitian idempotent using the matrix ``\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}`` and recover its complex scalar–vector coefficients.","output":"","result":"## Zero divisors, idempotents, and spectral projectors\n\nA nonzero element ``A`` is a **zero divisor** if it annihilates some nonzero element on at least one side. Since APS is isomorphic to complex two-by-two matrices, its nonzero noninvertible elements have rank one and are zero divisors. Equivalently, for ``A=z+\\mathbf w``,\n\n```math\nz^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w=0.\n```\n\nThe word *null* has referred to real paravectors and their Minkowski norm; a generic singular multivector need not be a real paravector.\n\n### Idempotents and complementary ideals\n\nAn **idempotent** satisfies ``f^2=f``. If ``\\mathbf n`` is a real unit spatial vector, define\n\n```math\nf_\\pm=\\frac{1\\pm\\mathbf n}{2}.\n```\n\nThen\n\n```math\nf_\\pm^2=f_\\pm,\\qquad f_+f_-=f_-f_+=0,\n\\qquad f_++f_-=1,\\qquad f_\\pm^\\dagger=f_\\pm.\n```\n\nTheir matrices are orthogonal rank-one projectors. These idempotents are **primitive**: they cannot be decomposed into a sum of two nonzero mutually annihilating idempotents. Every nontrivial idempotent in complex two-by-two matrices has rank one and is primitive, although it need not be Hermitian.\n\nWriting ``\\mathcal A=\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}``, right multiplication by ``f_\\pm`` splits the algebra into left ideals:\n\n```math\n\\mathcal A=\\mathcal A f_+\\oplus\\mathcal A f_-.\n```\n\nEach ideal is stable under left multiplication by arbitrary algebra elements. For ``\\mathbf n=e_3``, the two matrix ideals consist of matrices with only the first or only the second column nonzero. Each has complex dimension two and is a spinor module, not a subalgebra with the original identity.\n\nTo see minimality, take any nonzero first column ``v``. By choosing a complex linear map ``M`` with ``Mv=u``, left multiplication can turn it into any prescribed column ``u``. Thus a nonzero left ideal contained in the first-column ideal must contain the entire ideal. This supplies the algebraic justification for the spinor construction later in the chapter.\n\n### Spectral decomposition of real paravectors\n\nFor ``p=p^0+\\mathbf p`` with ``r=|\\mathbf p|>0``, set ``\\mathbf n=\\mathbf p/r``. Then\n\n```math\np=(p^0+r)f_++(p^0-r)f_-.\n```\n\nUnder the Pauli map this is precisely the Hermitian spectral theorem. The projectors are orthogonal, and the eigenvalues are real. For example,\n\n```math\np=3+2e_1,\n\\qquad\nf_\\pm=\\frac{1\\pm e_1}{2},\n\\qquad p=5f_++f_-.\n```\n\nWhen ``\\mathbf p=0``, the matrix is scalar: both eigenvalues coincide and no direction is selected. If both eigenvalues are positive, choose orthonormal eigenvectors ``u_\\pm`` and obtain\n\n```math\nP=\\psi_+\\psi_+^\\dagger+\\psi_-\\psi_-^\\dagger,\n\\qquad\n\\psi_\\pm=\\sqrt{p^0\\pm r}\\,u_\\pm.\n```\n\nThis proves the two-spinor decomposition of a future timelike paravector matrix.\n\n### Optional extension: general complex spectra and Jordan form\n\nFor a generic element ``A=z+\\mathbf w``, choose ``\\lambda\\in\\mathbb C_I`` with ``\\lambda^2=\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w``. If ``\\lambda\\ne0``, define\n\n```math\nF_\\pm=\\frac12\\left(1\\pm\\frac{\\mathbf w}{\\lambda}\\right).\n```\n\nThe identity ``\\mathbf w^2=\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w`` gives\n\n```math\nF_\\pm^2=F_\\pm,\\qquad F_+F_-=0,\n\\qquad A=(z+\\lambda)F_++(z-\\lambda)F_-.\n```\n\nThe projectors need not be Hermitian. Changing the square-root branch merely swaps their labels. The characteristic identity is\n\n```math\nA^2-2zA+(z^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w)1=0.\n```\n\nIf ``\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w=0`` but ``\\mathbf w\\ne0``, then ``\\mathbf w^2=0``: it is a nonzero nilpotent. The element ``A=z+\\mathbf w`` has a repeated eigenvalue and is not diagonalizable. An explicit example is\n\n```math\nN=e_1+Ie_2,\n\\qquad N^2=0,\n\\qquad \\rho(N)=\\begin{pmatrix}0&2\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nBy contrast, if ``\\mathbf w=0``, the element is scalar and diagonalizable. These are all the repeated-eigenvalue cases for two-by-two matrices.\n\nBeing singular, nilpotent, and idempotent are different properties. The singular paravector ``1+e_3`` has square ``2(1+e_3)`` and is neither idempotent nor nilpotent; its half is idempotent. Also ``1+N`` is invertible despite not being diagonalizable, with inverse ``1-N``.\n\n### Exercises on singular elements and spectra\n\n1. Verify ``(1+e_3)(1-e_3)=0`` and explain why neither factor can be inverted.\n2. Find the spectral projectors of ``3+e_1+2e_2+2e_3``. Check that its eigenvalues are ``6`` and ``0``.\n3. Show that ``(1+N)^{-1}=1-N`` and determine its determinant and repeated eigenvalue.\n4. Construct a non-Hermitian idempotent using the matrix ``\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}`` and recover its complex scalar–vector coefficients.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4770-zero-divisors-idempotents-and-spectral-projectors\">\n<h1>Zero divisors, idempotents, and spectral projectors</h1>\n<p>A nonzero element <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A\\)</span> is a <strong>zero divisor</strong> if it annihilates some nonzero element on at least one side. Since APS is isomorphic to complex two-by-two matrices, its nonzero noninvertible elements have rank one and are zero divisors. Equivalently, for <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A=z+\\mathbf w\\)</span>,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nz^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w=0.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The word <em>null</em> has referred to real paravectors and their Minkowski norm; a generic singular multivector need not be a real paravector.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4770-idempotents-and-complementary-ideals\">\n<h1>Idempotents and complementary ideals</h1>\n<p>An <strong>idempotent</strong> satisfies <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f^2=f\\)</span>. If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf n\\)</span> is a real unit spatial vector, define</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nf_\\pm=\\frac{1\\pm\\mathbf n}{2}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Then</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nf_\\pm^2=f_\\pm,\\qquad f_+f_-=f_-f_+=0,\n\\qquad f_++f_-=1,\\qquad f_\\pm^\\dagger=f_\\pm.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Their matrices are orthogonal rank-one projectors. These idempotents are <strong>primitive</strong>: they cannot be decomposed into a sum of two nonzero mutually annihilating idempotents. Every nontrivial idempotent in complex two-by-two matrices has rank one and is primitive, although it need not be Hermitian.</p>\n<p>Writing <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathcal A=\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\)</span>, right multiplication by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f_\\pm\\)</span> splits the algebra into left ideals:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathcal A=\\mathcal A f_+\\oplus\\mathcal A f_-.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Each ideal is stable under left multiplication by arbitrary algebra elements. For <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf n=e_3\\)</span>, the two matrix ideals consist of matrices with only the first or only the second column nonzero. Each has complex dimension two and is a spinor module, not a subalgebra with the original identity.</p>\n<p>To see minimality, take any nonzero first column <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(v\\)</span>. By choosing a complex linear map <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(M\\)</span> with <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(Mv=u\\)</span>, left multiplication can turn it into any prescribed column <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(u\\)</span>. Thus a nonzero left ideal contained in the first-column ideal must contain the entire ideal. This supplies the algebraic justification for the spinor construction later in the chapter.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4770-spectral-decomposition-of-real-paravectors\">\n<h1>Spectral decomposition of real paravectors</h1>\n<p>For <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(p=p^0+\\mathbf p\\)</span> with <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r=|\\mathbf p|&gt;0\\)</span>, set <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf n=\\mathbf p/r\\)</span>. Then</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np=(p^0+r)f_++(p^0-r)f_-.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Under the Pauli map this is precisely the Hermitian spectral theorem. The projectors are orthogonal, and the eigenvalues are real. For example,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np=3+2e_1,\n\\qquad\nf_\\pm=\\frac{1\\pm e_1}{2},\n\\qquad p=5f_++f_-.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>When <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf p=0\\)</span>, the matrix is scalar: both eigenvalues coincide and no direction is selected. If both eigenvalues are positive, choose orthonormal eigenvectors <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(u_\\pm\\)</span> and obtain</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nP=\\psi_+\\psi_+^\\dagger+\\psi_-\\psi_-^\\dagger,\n\\qquad\n\\psi_\\pm=\\sqrt{p^0\\pm r}\\,u_\\pm.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This proves the two-spinor decomposition of a future timelike paravector matrix.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4770-optional-extension-general-complex-spectra-and-jordan-form\">\n<h1>Optional extension: general complex spectra and Jordan form</h1>\n<p>For a generic element <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A=z+\\mathbf w\\)</span>, choose <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb C_I\\)</span> with <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lambda^2=\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w\\)</span>. If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lambda\\ne0\\)</span>, define</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nF_\\pm=\\frac12\\left(1\\pm\\frac{\\mathbf w}{\\lambda}\\right).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The identity <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf w^2=\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w\\)</span> gives</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nF_\\pm^2=F_\\pm,\\qquad F_+F_-=0,\n\\qquad A=(z+\\lambda)F_++(z-\\lambda)F_-.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The projectors need not be Hermitian. Changing the square-root branch merely swaps their labels. The characteristic identity is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA^2-2zA+(z^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w)1=0.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w=0\\)</span> but <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf w\\ne0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf w^2=0\\)</span>: it is a nonzero nilpotent. The element <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A=z+\\mathbf w\\)</span> has a repeated eigenvalue and is not diagonalizable. An explicit example is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nN=e_1+Ie_2,\n\\qquad N^2=0,\n\\qquad \\rho(N)=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;2\\\\0&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>By contrast, if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf w=0\\)</span>, the element is scalar and diagonalizable. These are all the repeated-eigenvalue cases for two-by-two matrices.</p>\n<p>Being singular, nilpotent, and idempotent are different properties. The singular paravector <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1+e_3\\)</span> has square <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(2(1+e_3)\\)</span> and is neither idempotent nor nilpotent; its half is idempotent. Also <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1+N\\)</span> is invertible despite not being diagonalizable, with inverse <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1-N\\)</span>.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4770-exercises-on-singular-elements-and-spectra\">\n<h1>Exercises on singular elements and spectra</h1>\n<ol>\n<li>Verify <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((1+e_3)(1-e_3)=0\\)</span> and explain why neither factor can be inverted.</li>\n<li>Find the spectral projectors of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(3+e_1+2e_2+2e_3\\)</span>. Check that its eigenvalues are <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(6\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>Show that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((1+N)^{-1}=1-N\\)</span> and determine its determinant and repeated eigenvalue.</li>\n<li>Construct a non-Hermitian idempotent using the matrix <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\0&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}\\)</span> and recover its complex scalar–vector coefficients.</li>\n</ol>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Rotations and Lorentz transformations\n\nLet ``L`` be a complex two-by-two matrix with determinant one. Transform a paravector matrix by the **sandwich action**\n\n```math\nP'=LPL^\\dagger,\n\\qquad L\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C).\n```\n\nThis preserves Hermiticity and the determinant:\n\n```math\n(P')^\\dagger=P',\n\\qquad\n\\det P'=\\det L\\,\\det P\\,\\overline{\\det L}=\\det P.\n```\n\nIt therefore defines a real linear transformation preserving the Minkowski norm. These transformations give precisely the proper orthochronous Lorentz group: spatial orientation and time orientation are preserved. The matrices ``L`` and ``-L`` produce the same action, so this is a two-to-one covering, not a one-to-one identification. Reflections and time reversal are not included.\n\nIn APS notation the same action is ``p'=\\ell p\\ell^\\dagger``, where ``\\rho(\\ell)=L`` and the dagger denotes reversion. A general Lorentz transformation element is not itself restricted to the paravector subspace.\n\n### Spatial rotations\n\nFor an active right-handed rotation through angle ``\\theta`` about a unit spatial axis ``\\mathbf n``, choose\n\n```math\nU=\\exp\\left(-\\frac{i\\theta}{2}\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right)\n=\\cos\\frac\\theta2\\,\\mathbf 1_2-i\\sin\\frac\\theta2\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n```\n\nSince ``U^\\dagger=U^{-1}``, the transformation ``P'=UPU^\\dagger`` leaves the scalar part fixed and rotates the spatial part. For example, rotation about the third axis sends\n\n```math\nU\\sigma_1U^\\dagger\n=\\cos\\theta\\,\\sigma_1+\\sin\\theta\\,\\sigma_2.\n```\n\nIts APS counterpart is the rotor ``\\ell=\\exp(-I\\mathbf n\\theta/2)``. The half-angle explains why a full turn changes the rotor's sign while leaving the represented vector unchanged.\n\n### Boosts\n\nAn active boost of rapidity ``\\eta`` along ``\\mathbf n`` is represented, with the sign convention used here, by\n\n```math\nB=\\exp\\left(\\frac\\eta2\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right)\n=\\cosh\\frac\\eta2\\,\\mathbf 1_2+\\sinh\\frac\\eta2\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n```\n\nThe hyperbolic functions arise because ``(\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)^2=\\mathbf 1_2``. Unlike a rotation matrix, ``B`` is Hermitian rather than unitary. Resolve ``\\mathbf p=p_\\parallel\\mathbf n+\\mathbf p_\\perp``. The sandwich action gives\n\n```math\np'^0=p^0\\cosh\\eta+p_\\parallel\\sinh\\eta,\n\\qquad\np'_\\parallel=p_\\parallel\\cosh\\eta+p^0\\sinh\\eta,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf p'_\\perp=\\mathbf p_\\perp.\n```\n\nHere ``\\beta=v/c=\\tanh\\eta`` and ``\\gamma=\\cosh\\eta``. A passive change to a frame moving in the positive axis direction uses the inverse boost, obtained by replacing ``\\eta`` with ``-\\eta``. This distinction accounts for the opposite signs often seen in textbook coordinate formulas.\n\nAs a check, boost the rest four-momentum ``P=mc\\mathbf 1_2``:\n\n```math\nP'=mc\\,BB^\\dagger\n=mc\\left(\\cosh\\eta\\,\\mathbf 1_2+\\sinh\\eta\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right).\n```\n\nThus ``E=\\gamma mc^2`` and ``\\mathbf p=\\gamma m\\mathbf v``, while ``\\det P'=m^2c^2`` remains unchanged.","output":"","result":"## Rotations and Lorentz transformations\n\nLet ``L`` be a complex two-by-two matrix with determinant one. Transform a paravector matrix by the **sandwich action**\n\n```math\nP'=LPL^\\dagger,\n\\qquad L\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C).\n```\n\nThis preserves Hermiticity and the determinant:\n\n```math\n(P')^\\dagger=P',\n\\qquad\n\\det P'=\\det L\\,\\det P\\,\\overline{\\det L}=\\det P.\n```\n\nIt therefore defines a real linear transformation preserving the Minkowski norm. These transformations give precisely the proper orthochronous Lorentz group: spatial orientation and time orientation are preserved. The matrices ``L`` and ``-L`` produce the same action, so this is a two-to-one covering, not a one-to-one identification. Reflections and time reversal are not included.\n\nIn APS notation the same action is ``p'=\\ell p\\ell^\\dagger``, where ``\\rho(\\ell)=L`` and the dagger denotes reversion. A general Lorentz transformation element is not itself restricted to the paravector subspace.\n\n### Spatial rotations\n\nFor an active right-handed rotation through angle ``\\theta`` about a unit spatial axis ``\\mathbf n``, choose\n\n```math\nU=\\exp\\left(-\\frac{i\\theta}{2}\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right)\n=\\cos\\frac\\theta2\\,\\mathbf 1_2-i\\sin\\frac\\theta2\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n```\n\nSince ``U^\\dagger=U^{-1}``, the transformation ``P'=UPU^\\dagger`` leaves the scalar part fixed and rotates the spatial part. For example, rotation about the third axis sends\n\n```math\nU\\sigma_1U^\\dagger\n=\\cos\\theta\\,\\sigma_1+\\sin\\theta\\,\\sigma_2.\n```\n\nIts APS counterpart is the rotor ``\\ell=\\exp(-I\\mathbf n\\theta/2)``. The half-angle explains why a full turn changes the rotor's sign while leaving the represented vector unchanged.\n\n### Boosts\n\nAn active boost of rapidity ``\\eta`` along ``\\mathbf n`` is represented, with the sign convention used here, by\n\n```math\nB=\\exp\\left(\\frac\\eta2\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right)\n=\\cosh\\frac\\eta2\\,\\mathbf 1_2+\\sinh\\frac\\eta2\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n```\n\nThe hyperbolic functions arise because ``(\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)^2=\\mathbf 1_2``. Unlike a rotation matrix, ``B`` is Hermitian rather than unitary. Resolve ``\\mathbf p=p_\\parallel\\mathbf n+\\mathbf p_\\perp``. The sandwich action gives\n\n```math\np'^0=p^0\\cosh\\eta+p_\\parallel\\sinh\\eta,\n\\qquad\np'_\\parallel=p_\\parallel\\cosh\\eta+p^0\\sinh\\eta,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf p'_\\perp=\\mathbf p_\\perp.\n```\n\nHere ``\\beta=v/c=\\tanh\\eta`` and ``\\gamma=\\cosh\\eta``. A passive change to a frame moving in the positive axis direction uses the inverse boost, obtained by replacing ``\\eta`` with ``-\\eta``. This distinction accounts for the opposite signs often seen in textbook coordinate formulas.\n\nAs a check, boost the rest four-momentum ``P=mc\\mathbf 1_2``:\n\n```math\nP'=mc\\,BB^\\dagger\n=mc\\left(\\cosh\\eta\\,\\mathbf 1_2+\\sinh\\eta\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right).\n```\n\nThus ``E=\\gamma mc^2`` and ``\\mathbf p=\\gamma m\\mathbf v``, while ``\\det P'=m^2c^2`` remains unchanged.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4761-rotations-and-lorentz-transformations\">\n<h1>Rotations and Lorentz transformations</h1>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span> be a complex two-by-two matrix with determinant one. Transform a paravector matrix by the <strong>sandwich action</strong></p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nP'=LPL^\\dagger,\n\\qquad L\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This preserves Hermiticity and the determinant:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n(P')^\\dagger=P',\n\\qquad\n\\det P'=\\det L\\,\\det P\\,\\overline{\\det L}=\\det P.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>It therefore defines a real linear transformation preserving the Minkowski norm. These transformations give precisely the proper orthochronous Lorentz group: spatial orientation and time orientation are preserved. The matrices <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-L\\)</span> produce the same action, so this is a two-to-one covering, not a one-to-one identification. Reflections and time reversal are not included.</p>\n<p>In APS notation the same action is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(p'=\\ell p\\ell^\\dagger\\)</span>, where <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\rho(\\ell)=L\\)</span> and the dagger denotes reversion. A general Lorentz transformation element is not itself restricted to the paravector subspace.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4761-spatial-rotations\">\n<h1>Spatial rotations</h1>\n<p>For an active right-handed rotation through angle <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\theta\\)</span> about a unit spatial axis <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf n\\)</span>, choose</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nU=\\exp\\left(-\\frac{i\\theta}{2}\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right)\n=\\cos\\frac\\theta2\\,\\mathbf 1_2-i\\sin\\frac\\theta2\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(U^\\dagger=U^{-1}\\)</span>, the transformation <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P'=UPU^\\dagger\\)</span> leaves the scalar part fixed and rotates the spatial part. For example, rotation about the third axis sends</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nU\\sigma_1U^\\dagger\n=\\cos\\theta\\,\\sigma_1+\\sin\\theta\\,\\sigma_2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Its APS counterpart is the rotor <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ell=\\exp(-I\\mathbf n\\theta/2)\\)</span>. The half-angle explains why a full turn changes the rotor’s sign while leaving the represented vector unchanged.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4761-boosts\">\n<h1>Boosts</h1>\n<p>An active boost of rapidity <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\eta\\)</span> along <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf n\\)</span> is represented, with the sign convention used here, by</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nB=\\exp\\left(\\frac\\eta2\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right)\n=\\cosh\\frac\\eta2\\,\\mathbf 1_2+\\sinh\\frac\\eta2\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The hyperbolic functions arise because <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)^2=\\mathbf 1_2\\)</span>. Unlike a rotation matrix, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(B\\)</span> is Hermitian rather than unitary. Resolve <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf p=p_\\parallel\\mathbf n+\\mathbf p_\\perp\\)</span>. The sandwich action gives</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np'^0=p^0\\cosh\\eta+p_\\parallel\\sinh\\eta,\n\\qquad\np'_\\parallel=p_\\parallel\\cosh\\eta+p^0\\sinh\\eta,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf p'_\\perp=\\mathbf p_\\perp.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Here <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\beta=v/c=\\tanh\\eta\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\gamma=\\cosh\\eta\\)</span>. A passive change to a frame moving in the positive axis direction uses the inverse boost, obtained by replacing <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\eta\\)</span> with <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-\\eta\\)</span>. This distinction accounts for the opposite signs often seen in textbook coordinate formulas.</p>\n<p>As a check, boost the rest four-momentum <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P=mc\\mathbf 1_2\\)</span>:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nP'=mc\\,BB^\\dagger\n=mc\\left(\\cosh\\eta\\,\\mathbf 1_2+\\sinh\\eta\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Thus <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(E=\\gamma mc^2\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf p=\\gamma m\\mathbf v\\)</span>, while <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\det P'=m^2c^2\\)</span> remains unchanged.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Commutators and Lie algebras\n\nAn associative algebra also defines a Lie bracket through the commutator:\n\n```math\n[A,C]=AC-CA.\n```\n\nAssociativity implies the Jacobi identity. For complex scalar–vector elements the central scalar parts drop out:\n\n```math\n[z+\\mathbf w,s+\\mathbf u]=2I\\mathbf w\\times\\mathbf u.\n```\n\nThe complex vectors therefore form a three-dimensional complex Lie algebra. Under the Pauli map they are exactly the traceless matrices, giving ``\\mathfrak{sl}(2,\\mathbb C)``. Viewed over the reals this algebra has dimension six. The full associative APS algebra has complex dimension four; it must not be identified with its traceless commutator subalgebra.\n\n### Generators consistent with the sandwich convention\n\nDefine real rotation and boost generators within APS by\n\n```math\nJ_j=-\\frac12Ie_j,\\qquad K_j=\\frac12e_j.\n```\n\nTheir matrices are ``-i\\sigma_j/2`` and ``\\sigma_j/2``, respectively. Their signs match the active rotations and boosts already used. Since ``[e_j,e_k]=2I\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}e_\\ell``, direct substitution gives\n\n```math\n[J_j,J_k]=\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}J_\\ell,\n\\qquad\n[J_j,K_k]=\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}K_\\ell,\n\\qquad\n[K_j,K_k]=-\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}J_\\ell.\n```\n\nFor example, ``[K_1,K_2]=Ie_3/2=-J_3``. The real span of the ``J_j`` is ``\\mathfrak{su}(2)``, the Lie algebra of unit quaternions. Adding the ``K_j`` gives the real Lie algebra of the Lorentz spin group. Boost generators alone do not form a Lie subalgebra because their commutators produce rotations.\n\n### Infinitesimal action on paravectors\n\nLet ``\\ell(t)=1+tG+O(t^2)``. Differentiating the sandwich action yields\n\n```math\n\\delta p=Gp+pG^\\dagger.\n```\n\nFor ``G=-I\\mathbf a/2``, where ``\\mathbf a`` is a real infinitesimal rotation vector, ``G^\\dagger=-G``. For ``G=\\mathbf b/2``, where ``\\mathbf b`` is a real infinitesimal boost parameter, ``G^\\dagger=G``. Hence\n\n```math\n\\delta p^0=\\mathbf b\\cdot\\mathbf p,\n\\qquad\n\\delta\\mathbf p=\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf p+p^0\\mathbf b.\n```\n\nThese equations also check the generator signs without relying on a particular matrix basis. They imply ``\\delta[(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2]=0`` directly. Note that only the rotation action reduces to a commutator with ``p``; the boost action uses an anticommutator.\n\nA small group commutator makes noncommutativity concrete:\n\n```math\n(1+sG)(1+tH)(1-sG)(1-tH)\n=1+st[G,H]+O(s^2,t^2),\n```\n\nwhere only terms through first order in each parameter are displayed. More precisely, using exact group elements and their inverses gives a leading mixed term ``st[G,H]`` with higher terms of orders ``s^2t`` and ``st^2``. Two small boosts along distinct axes therefore generate a rotation at mixed order.\n\n### Lie-algebra exercises\n\n1. Derive the three generator brackets and verify one Jacobi identity involving two boosts and a rotation.\n2. Show that the infinitesimal component formulas preserve the Minkowski quadratic form.\n3. Prove that the trace of every matrix commutator vanishes and explain why the complex-vector subspace is closed under the bracket.","output":"","result":"## Commutators and Lie algebras\n\nAn associative algebra also defines a Lie bracket through the commutator:\n\n```math\n[A,C]=AC-CA.\n```\n\nAssociativity implies the Jacobi identity. For complex scalar–vector elements the central scalar parts drop out:\n\n```math\n[z+\\mathbf w,s+\\mathbf u]=2I\\mathbf w\\times\\mathbf u.\n```\n\nThe complex vectors therefore form a three-dimensional complex Lie algebra. Under the Pauli map they are exactly the traceless matrices, giving ``\\mathfrak{sl}(2,\\mathbb C)``. Viewed over the reals this algebra has dimension six. The full associative APS algebra has complex dimension four; it must not be identified with its traceless commutator subalgebra.\n\n### Generators consistent with the sandwich convention\n\nDefine real rotation and boost generators within APS by\n\n```math\nJ_j=-\\frac12Ie_j,\\qquad K_j=\\frac12e_j.\n```\n\nTheir matrices are ``-i\\sigma_j/2`` and ``\\sigma_j/2``, respectively. Their signs match the active rotations and boosts already used. Since ``[e_j,e_k]=2I\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}e_\\ell``, direct substitution gives\n\n```math\n[J_j,J_k]=\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}J_\\ell,\n\\qquad\n[J_j,K_k]=\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}K_\\ell,\n\\qquad\n[K_j,K_k]=-\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}J_\\ell.\n```\n\nFor example, ``[K_1,K_2]=Ie_3/2=-J_3``. The real span of the ``J_j`` is ``\\mathfrak{su}(2)``, the Lie algebra of unit quaternions. Adding the ``K_j`` gives the real Lie algebra of the Lorentz spin group. Boost generators alone do not form a Lie subalgebra because their commutators produce rotations.\n\n### Infinitesimal action on paravectors\n\nLet ``\\ell(t)=1+tG+O(t^2)``. Differentiating the sandwich action yields\n\n```math\n\\delta p=Gp+pG^\\dagger.\n```\n\nFor ``G=-I\\mathbf a/2``, where ``\\mathbf a`` is a real infinitesimal rotation vector, ``G^\\dagger=-G``. For ``G=\\mathbf b/2``, where ``\\mathbf b`` is a real infinitesimal boost parameter, ``G^\\dagger=G``. Hence\n\n```math\n\\delta p^0=\\mathbf b\\cdot\\mathbf p,\n\\qquad\n\\delta\\mathbf p=\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf p+p^0\\mathbf b.\n```\n\nThese equations also check the generator signs without relying on a particular matrix basis. They imply ``\\delta[(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2]=0`` directly. Note that only the rotation action reduces to a commutator with ``p``; the boost action uses an anticommutator.\n\nA small group commutator makes noncommutativity concrete:\n\n```math\n(1+sG)(1+tH)(1-sG)(1-tH)\n=1+st[G,H]+O(s^2,t^2),\n```\n\nwhere only terms through first order in each parameter are displayed. More precisely, using exact group elements and their inverses gives a leading mixed term ``st[G,H]`` with higher terms of orders ``s^2t`` and ``st^2``. Two small boosts along distinct axes therefore generate a rotation at mixed order.\n\n### Lie-algebra exercises\n\n1. Derive the three generator brackets and verify one Jacobi identity involving two boosts and a rotation.\n2. Show that the infinitesimal component formulas preserve the Minkowski quadratic form.\n3. Prove that the trace of every matrix commutator vanishes and explain why the complex-vector subspace is closed under the bracket.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4771-commutators-and-lie-algebras\">\n<h1>Commutators and Lie algebras</h1>\n<p>An associative algebra also defines a Lie bracket through the commutator:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n[A,C]=AC-CA.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Associativity implies the Jacobi identity. For complex scalar–vector elements the central scalar parts drop out:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n[z+\\mathbf w,s+\\mathbf u]=2I\\mathbf w\\times\\mathbf u.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The complex vectors therefore form a three-dimensional complex Lie algebra. Under the Pauli map they are exactly the traceless matrices, giving <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathfrak{sl}(2,\\mathbb C)\\)</span>. Viewed over the reals this algebra has dimension six. The full associative APS algebra has complex dimension four; it must not be identified with its traceless commutator subalgebra.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4771-generators-consistent-with-the-sandwich-convention\">\n<h1>Generators consistent with the sandwich convention</h1>\n<p>Define real rotation and boost generators within APS by</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nJ_j=-\\frac12Ie_j,\\qquad K_j=\\frac12e_j.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Their matrices are <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-i\\sigma_j/2\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\sigma_j/2\\)</span>, respectively. Their signs match the active rotations and boosts already used. Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\([e_j,e_k]=2I\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}e_\\ell\\)</span>, direct substitution gives</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n[J_j,J_k]=\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}J_\\ell,\n\\qquad\n[J_j,K_k]=\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}K_\\ell,\n\\qquad\n[K_j,K_k]=-\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}J_\\ell.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For example, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\([K_1,K_2]=Ie_3/2=-J_3\\)</span>. The real span of the <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(J_j\\)</span> is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathfrak{su}(2)\\)</span>, the Lie algebra of unit quaternions. Adding the <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(K_j\\)</span> gives the real Lie algebra of the Lorentz spin group. Boost generators alone do not form a Lie subalgebra because their commutators produce rotations.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4771-infinitesimal-action-on-paravectors\">\n<h1>Infinitesimal action on paravectors</h1>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ell(t)=1+tG+O(t^2)\\)</span>. Differentiating the sandwich action yields</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\delta p=Gp+pG^\\dagger.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(G=-I\\mathbf a/2\\)</span>, where <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf a\\)</span> is a real infinitesimal rotation vector, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(G^\\dagger=-G\\)</span>. For <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(G=\\mathbf b/2\\)</span>, where <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf b\\)</span> is a real infinitesimal boost parameter, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(G^\\dagger=G\\)</span>. Hence</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\delta p^0=\\mathbf b\\cdot\\mathbf p,\n\\qquad\n\\delta\\mathbf p=\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf p+p^0\\mathbf b.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>These equations also check the generator signs without relying on a particular matrix basis. They imply <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta[(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2]=0\\)</span> directly. Note that only the rotation action reduces to a commutator with <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(p\\)</span>; the boost action uses an anticommutator.</p>\n<p>A small group commutator makes noncommutativity concrete:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n(1+sG)(1+tH)(1-sG)(1-tH)\n=1+st[G,H]+O(s^2,t^2),\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>where only terms through first order in each parameter are displayed. More precisely, using exact group elements and their inverses gives a leading mixed term <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(st[G,H]\\)</span> with higher terms of orders <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(s^2t\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(st^2\\)</span>. Two small boosts along distinct axes therefore generate a rotation at mixed order.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4771-liealgebra-exercises\">\n<h1>Lie-algebra exercises</h1>\n<ol>\n<li>Derive the three generator brackets and verify one Jacobi identity involving two boosts and a rotation.</li>\n<li>Show that the infinitesimal component formulas preserve the Minkowski quadratic form.</li>\n<li>Prove that the trace of every matrix commutator vanishes and explain why the complex-vector subspace is closed under the bracket.</li>\n</ol>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Spinors and their relation to paravectors\n\nA **spinor** is characterized by how it transforms, not simply by having complex components. The most direct example here is a two-component column\n\n```math\n\\psi=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha\\\\\\beta\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathbb C^2,\n\\qquad \\psi'=L\\psi,\n\\qquad L\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C).\n```\n\nThis is one of the two chiral Weyl-spinor representations of the proper Lorentz spin group. Under spatial rotations, ``L=U\\in\\mathrm{SU}(2)``, it is the familiar Pauli spinor. A spinor transforms by **one-sided multiplication**, whereas a paravector matrix transforms by ``P'=LPL^\\dagger``. A two-component spinor has four real components, but it is not a four-vector: its transformation law is different.\n\n### Why the half-angle matters\n\nFor a rotation about the third axis,\n\n```math\nU(\\theta)=\\begin{pmatrix}e^{-i\\theta/2}&0\\\\0&e^{i\\theta/2}\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\n\\psi'=\\begin{pmatrix}e^{-i\\theta/2}\\alpha\\\\e^{i\\theta/2}\\beta\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nConsequently,\n\n```math\nU(2\\pi)=-\\mathbf 1_2,\\qquad U(4\\pi)=\\mathbf 1_2.\n```\n\nA full spatial turn sends a spinor to its negative, while leaving its vector bilinears unchanged. In quantum mechanics an overall phase does not change an isolated pure state, but a relative sign between coherently compared amplitudes can be observable. The sign change expresses the double covering of the rotation group; it is not a failure of ordinary vectors to return after a full turn.\n\n### Building a null paravector from a spinor\n\nThe outer product of a nonzero spinor with its Hermitian conjugate is\n\n```math\nK=\\psi\\psi^\\dagger\n=\\begin{pmatrix}|\\alpha|^2&\\alpha\\beta^*\\\\\\beta\\alpha^*&|\\beta|^2\\end{pmatrix}\n=k^0\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf k\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n```\n\nReading off the Pauli coefficients gives\n\n```math\nk^0=\\frac{|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2}{2},\\qquad\nk^1=\\operatorname{Re}(\\alpha^*\\beta),\\qquad\nk^2=\\operatorname{Im}(\\alpha^*\\beta),\\qquad\nk^3=\\frac{|\\alpha|^2-|\\beta|^2}{2}.\n```\n\nThe matrix has rank one, so\n\n```math\n\\det K=(k^0)^2-|\\mathbf k|^2=0,\\qquad k^0>0.\n```\n\nIt therefore represents a future-directed null paravector. Moreover,\n\n```math\nK'=(L\\psi)(L\\psi)^\\dagger=LKL^\\dagger,\n```\n\nwhich derives the vector transformation law from the spinor law. Every future-directed nonzero null paravector admits this factorization, unique up to ``\\psi\\mapsto e^{i\\chi}\\psi``. Multiplying the spinor by a general nonzero complex number also rescales the null paravector; after ignoring positive scale, complex spinor rays label directions on the celestial sphere.\n\nA single such outer product cannot represent a timelike paravector. A positive-definite paravector matrix can instead be expressed as a sum of two independent spinor outer products, for example by its spectral decomposition.\n\n### Normalized Pauli spinors and the Bloch sphere\n\nIn a fixed observer's frame, normalize ``\\psi^\\dagger\\psi=1`` and define\n\n```math\nn_j=\\psi^\\dagger\\sigma_j\\psi,\n\\qquad\n\\psi\\psi^\\dagger=\\frac12\\left(\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right),\n\\qquad |\\mathbf n|=1.\n```\n\nThus a normalized spinor determines a unit spatial direction, with its overall phase discarded. An explicit representative is\n\n```math\n\\psi(\\theta,\\phi)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos(\\theta/2)\\\\e^{i\\phi}\\sin(\\theta/2)\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf n=(\\sin\\theta\\cos\\phi,\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi,\\cos\\theta).\n```\n\nFor example, ``\\psi=(1,1)^T/\\sqrt2`` gives ``\\mathbf n=e_1``. For a spin-one-half pure state, ``\\langle\\mathbf S\\rangle=(\\hbar/2)\\mathbf n``. These expectation values use the Hermitian product, not the complex-bilinear dot product of the preceding algebraic section. Rotations preserve the normalization; general boosts do not, although they preserve the null character of ``K``.\n\n### Spinors inside APS: a minimal left ideal\n\nThe column representation can be realized intrinsically in the algebra. Choose the reference idempotent\n\n```math\nf=\\frac{1+e_3}{2},\\qquad f^2=f,\n\\qquad \\rho(f)=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThe set ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f=\\{Af:A\\in\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\}`` is a **minimal left ideal**: it is closed under left multiplication by the algebra and contains no smaller nonzero left ideal. Its elements have the unique form\n\n```math\n\\Psi=(\\alpha+\\beta e_1)f,\n\\qquad \\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb C_I.\n```\n\nIdentifying ``I`` with ``i``, their matrices have only a first column:\n\n```math\n\\rho(\\Psi)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha&0\\\\\\beta&0\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThat first column is exactly the two-component spinor. If ``\\rho(\\ell)=L``, the algebraic transformation ``\\Psi'=\\ell\\Psi`` stays in the ideal and reproduces ``\\psi'=L\\psi``. The bilinear ``\\Psi\\Psi^\\dagger`` represents the null paravector encoded by ``\\psi\\psi^\\dagger``. Choosing ``f`` selects a reference spin axis; it does not select a preferred direction in the physical laws.\n\n### Rotor representatives and terminology\n\nFor spatial rotations, every normalized Pauli spinor can also be represented as ``\\Psi=Rf``, where ``R`` is a unit even element of APS. An explicit matrix representative is\n\n```math\n\\rho(R)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha&-\\beta^*\\\\\\beta&\\alpha^*\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathrm{SU}(2),\n\\qquad |\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1.\n```\n\nIts associated direction satisfies\n\n```math\n\\mathbf n=Re_3R^\\dagger,\n\\qquad\nRfR^\\dagger=\\frac{1+\\mathbf n}{2}.\n```\n\nLeft multiplication rotates the representative: ``R'=SR``. Right multiplication by ``\\exp(-Ie_3\\chi/2)`` leaves ``\\mathbf n`` unchanged but multiplies the ideal spinor by the phase ``e^{-I\\chi/2}``. Thus the direction alone does not determine the full spinor phase.\n\nThe word *spinor* is also used in APS for transformation-carrying algebra elements such as rotor or Lorentz-frame representatives. These must be distinguished from minimal-ideal spinors: a determinant-one Lorentz representative has six real parameters and encodes a frame, whereas a two-component Weyl spinor has four real components. A column spinor is not itself an invertible Lorentz transformation.\n\nFinally, the opposite chiral Lorentz spinor transforms as ``\\chi'=(L^\\dagger)^{-1}\\chi``. A Dirac spinor combines both chiral types. The two-component construction here is therefore a foundation for relativistic spinors, not by itself a complete massive Dirac wavefunction.\n\n### Spinor exercises\n\n1. Verify the four component formulas for ``K`` and check directly that its Minkowski norm vanishes.\n2. Show that the normalized spinors ``(1,0)^T``, ``(0,1)^T``, and ``(1,i)^T/\\sqrt2`` give the directions ``e_3``, ``-e_3``, and ``e_2``, respectively.\n3. Apply the boost ``B=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{\\eta/2},e^{-\\eta/2})`` to ``\\psi=(1,0)^T``. Show that ``K`` is multiplied by ``e^\\eta`` and remains null.\n4. Verify ``f^2=f`` and ``e_3f=f``. Explain why left multiplication by any APS element preserves the set ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f``.","output":"","result":"## Spinors and their relation to paravectors\n\nA **spinor** is characterized by how it transforms, not simply by having complex components. The most direct example here is a two-component column\n\n```math\n\\psi=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha\\\\\\beta\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathbb C^2,\n\\qquad \\psi'=L\\psi,\n\\qquad L\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C).\n```\n\nThis is one of the two chiral Weyl-spinor representations of the proper Lorentz spin group. Under spatial rotations, ``L=U\\in\\mathrm{SU}(2)``, it is the familiar Pauli spinor. A spinor transforms by **one-sided multiplication**, whereas a paravector matrix transforms by ``P'=LPL^\\dagger``. A two-component spinor has four real components, but it is not a four-vector: its transformation law is different.\n\n### Why the half-angle matters\n\nFor a rotation about the third axis,\n\n```math\nU(\\theta)=\\begin{pmatrix}e^{-i\\theta/2}&0\\\\0&e^{i\\theta/2}\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\n\\psi'=\\begin{pmatrix}e^{-i\\theta/2}\\alpha\\\\e^{i\\theta/2}\\beta\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nConsequently,\n\n```math\nU(2\\pi)=-\\mathbf 1_2,\\qquad U(4\\pi)=\\mathbf 1_2.\n```\n\nA full spatial turn sends a spinor to its negative, while leaving its vector bilinears unchanged. In quantum mechanics an overall phase does not change an isolated pure state, but a relative sign between coherently compared amplitudes can be observable. The sign change expresses the double covering of the rotation group; it is not a failure of ordinary vectors to return after a full turn.\n\n### Building a null paravector from a spinor\n\nThe outer product of a nonzero spinor with its Hermitian conjugate is\n\n```math\nK=\\psi\\psi^\\dagger\n=\\begin{pmatrix}|\\alpha|^2&\\alpha\\beta^*\\\\\\beta\\alpha^*&|\\beta|^2\\end{pmatrix}\n=k^0\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf k\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n```\n\nReading off the Pauli coefficients gives\n\n```math\nk^0=\\frac{|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2}{2},\\qquad\nk^1=\\operatorname{Re}(\\alpha^*\\beta),\\qquad\nk^2=\\operatorname{Im}(\\alpha^*\\beta),\\qquad\nk^3=\\frac{|\\alpha|^2-|\\beta|^2}{2}.\n```\n\nThe matrix has rank one, so\n\n```math\n\\det K=(k^0)^2-|\\mathbf k|^2=0,\\qquad k^0>0.\n```\n\nIt therefore represents a future-directed null paravector. Moreover,\n\n```math\nK'=(L\\psi)(L\\psi)^\\dagger=LKL^\\dagger,\n```\n\nwhich derives the vector transformation law from the spinor law. Every future-directed nonzero null paravector admits this factorization, unique up to ``\\psi\\mapsto e^{i\\chi}\\psi``. Multiplying the spinor by a general nonzero complex number also rescales the null paravector; after ignoring positive scale, complex spinor rays label directions on the celestial sphere.\n\nA single such outer product cannot represent a timelike paravector. A positive-definite paravector matrix can instead be expressed as a sum of two independent spinor outer products, for example by its spectral decomposition.\n\n### Normalized Pauli spinors and the Bloch sphere\n\nIn a fixed observer's frame, normalize ``\\psi^\\dagger\\psi=1`` and define\n\n```math\nn_j=\\psi^\\dagger\\sigma_j\\psi,\n\\qquad\n\\psi\\psi^\\dagger=\\frac12\\left(\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right),\n\\qquad |\\mathbf n|=1.\n```\n\nThus a normalized spinor determines a unit spatial direction, with its overall phase discarded. An explicit representative is\n\n```math\n\\psi(\\theta,\\phi)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos(\\theta/2)\\\\e^{i\\phi}\\sin(\\theta/2)\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf n=(\\sin\\theta\\cos\\phi,\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi,\\cos\\theta).\n```\n\nFor example, ``\\psi=(1,1)^T/\\sqrt2`` gives ``\\mathbf n=e_1``. For a spin-one-half pure state, ``\\langle\\mathbf S\\rangle=(\\hbar/2)\\mathbf n``. These expectation values use the Hermitian product, not the complex-bilinear dot product of the preceding algebraic section. Rotations preserve the normalization; general boosts do not, although they preserve the null character of ``K``.\n\n### Spinors inside APS: a minimal left ideal\n\nThe column representation can be realized intrinsically in the algebra. Choose the reference idempotent\n\n```math\nf=\\frac{1+e_3}{2},\\qquad f^2=f,\n\\qquad \\rho(f)=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThe set ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f=\\{Af:A\\in\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\}`` is a **minimal left ideal**: it is closed under left multiplication by the algebra and contains no smaller nonzero left ideal. Its elements have the unique form\n\n```math\n\\Psi=(\\alpha+\\beta e_1)f,\n\\qquad \\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb C_I.\n```\n\nIdentifying ``I`` with ``i``, their matrices have only a first column:\n\n```math\n\\rho(\\Psi)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha&0\\\\\\beta&0\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThat first column is exactly the two-component spinor. If ``\\rho(\\ell)=L``, the algebraic transformation ``\\Psi'=\\ell\\Psi`` stays in the ideal and reproduces ``\\psi'=L\\psi``. The bilinear ``\\Psi\\Psi^\\dagger`` represents the null paravector encoded by ``\\psi\\psi^\\dagger``. Choosing ``f`` selects a reference spin axis; it does not select a preferred direction in the physical laws.\n\n### Rotor representatives and terminology\n\nFor spatial rotations, every normalized Pauli spinor can also be represented as ``\\Psi=Rf``, where ``R`` is a unit even element of APS. An explicit matrix representative is\n\n```math\n\\rho(R)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha&-\\beta^*\\\\\\beta&\\alpha^*\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathrm{SU}(2),\n\\qquad |\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1.\n```\n\nIts associated direction satisfies\n\n```math\n\\mathbf n=Re_3R^\\dagger,\n\\qquad\nRfR^\\dagger=\\frac{1+\\mathbf n}{2}.\n```\n\nLeft multiplication rotates the representative: ``R'=SR``. Right multiplication by ``\\exp(-Ie_3\\chi/2)`` leaves ``\\mathbf n`` unchanged but multiplies the ideal spinor by the phase ``e^{-I\\chi/2}``. Thus the direction alone does not determine the full spinor phase.\n\nThe word *spinor* is also used in APS for transformation-carrying algebra elements such as rotor or Lorentz-frame representatives. These must be distinguished from minimal-ideal spinors: a determinant-one Lorentz representative has six real parameters and encodes a frame, whereas a two-component Weyl spinor has four real components. A column spinor is not itself an invertible Lorentz transformation.\n\nFinally, the opposite chiral Lorentz spinor transforms as ``\\chi'=(L^\\dagger)^{-1}\\chi``. A Dirac spinor combines both chiral types. The two-component construction here is therefore a foundation for relativistic spinors, not by itself a complete massive Dirac wavefunction.\n\n### Spinor exercises\n\n1. Verify the four component formulas for ``K`` and check directly that its Minkowski norm vanishes.\n2. Show that the normalized spinors ``(1,0)^T``, ``(0,1)^T``, and ``(1,i)^T/\\sqrt2`` give the directions ``e_3``, ``-e_3``, and ``e_2``, respectively.\n3. Apply the boost ``B=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{\\eta/2},e^{-\\eta/2})`` to ``\\psi=(1,0)^T``. Show that ``K`` is multiplied by ``e^\\eta`` and remains null.\n4. Verify ``f^2=f`` and ``e_3f=f``. Explain why left multiplication by any APS element preserves the set ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f``.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4767-spinors-and-their-relation-to-paravectors\">\n<h1>Spinors and their relation to paravectors</h1>\n<p>A <strong>spinor</strong> is characterized by how it transforms, not simply by having complex components. The most direct example here is a two-component column</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\psi=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha\\\\\\beta\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathbb C^2,\n\\qquad \\psi'=L\\psi,\n\\qquad L\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This is one of the two chiral Weyl-spinor representations of the proper Lorentz spin group. Under spatial rotations, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L=U\\in\\mathrm{SU}(2)\\)</span>, it is the familiar Pauli spinor. A spinor transforms by <strong>one-sided multiplication</strong>, whereas a paravector matrix transforms by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P'=LPL^\\dagger\\)</span>. A two-component spinor has four real components, but it is not a four-vector: its transformation law is different.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4767-why-the-halfangle-matters\">\n<h1>Why the half-angle matters</h1>\n<p>For a rotation about the third axis,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nU(\\theta)=\\begin{pmatrix}e^{-i\\theta/2}&amp;0\\\\0&amp;e^{i\\theta/2}\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\n\\psi'=\\begin{pmatrix}e^{-i\\theta/2}\\alpha\\\\e^{i\\theta/2}\\beta\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Consequently,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nU(2\\pi)=-\\mathbf 1_2,\\qquad U(4\\pi)=\\mathbf 1_2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>A full spatial turn sends a spinor to its negative, while leaving its vector bilinears unchanged. In quantum mechanics an overall phase does not change an isolated pure state, but a relative sign between coherently compared amplitudes can be observable. The sign change expresses the double covering of the rotation group; it is not a failure of ordinary vectors to return after a full turn.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4767-building-a-null-paravector-from-a-spinor\">\n<h1>Building a null paravector from a spinor</h1>\n<p>The outer product of a nonzero spinor with its Hermitian conjugate is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nK=\\psi\\psi^\\dagger\n=\\begin{pmatrix}|\\alpha|^2&amp;\\alpha\\beta^*\\\\\\beta\\alpha^*&amp;|\\beta|^2\\end{pmatrix}\n=k^0\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf k\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Reading off the Pauli coefficients gives</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nk^0=\\frac{|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2}{2},\\qquad\nk^1=\\operatorname{Re}(\\alpha^*\\beta),\\qquad\nk^2=\\operatorname{Im}(\\alpha^*\\beta),\\qquad\nk^3=\\frac{|\\alpha|^2-|\\beta|^2}{2}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The matrix has rank one, so</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\det K=(k^0)^2-|\\mathbf k|^2=0,\\qquad k^0&gt;0.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>It therefore represents a future-directed null paravector. Moreover,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nK'=(L\\psi)(L\\psi)^\\dagger=LKL^\\dagger,\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>which derives the vector transformation law from the spinor law. Every future-directed nonzero null paravector admits this factorization, unique up to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\psi\\mapsto e^{i\\chi}\\psi\\)</span>. Multiplying the spinor by a general nonzero complex number also rescales the null paravector; after ignoring positive scale, complex spinor rays label directions on the celestial sphere.</p>\n<p>A single such outer product cannot represent a timelike paravector. A positive-definite paravector matrix can instead be expressed as a sum of two independent spinor outer products, for example by its spectral decomposition.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4767-normalized-pauli-spinors-and-the-bloch-sphere\">\n<h1>Normalized Pauli spinors and the Bloch sphere</h1>\n<p>In a fixed observer’s frame, normalize <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\psi^\\dagger\\psi=1\\)</span> and define</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nn_j=\\psi^\\dagger\\sigma_j\\psi,\n\\qquad\n\\psi\\psi^\\dagger=\\frac12\\left(\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right),\n\\qquad |\\mathbf n|=1.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Thus a normalized spinor determines a unit spatial direction, with its overall phase discarded. An explicit representative is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\psi(\\theta,\\phi)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos(\\theta/2)\\\\e^{i\\phi}\\sin(\\theta/2)\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf n=(\\sin\\theta\\cos\\phi,\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi,\\cos\\theta).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For example, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\psi=(1,1)^T/\\sqrt2\\)</span> gives <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf n=e_1\\)</span>. For a spin-one-half pure state, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\langle\\mathbf S\\rangle=(\\hbar/2)\\mathbf n\\)</span>. These expectation values use the Hermitian product, not the complex-bilinear dot product of the preceding algebraic section. Rotations preserve the normalization; general boosts do not, although they preserve the null character of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(K\\)</span>.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4767-spinors-inside-aps-a-minimal-left-ideal\">\n<h1>Spinors inside APS: a minimal left ideal</h1>\n<p>The column representation can be realized intrinsically in the algebra. Choose the reference idempotent</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nf=\\frac{1+e_3}{2},\\qquad f^2=f,\n\\qquad \\rho(f)=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The set <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f=\\{Af:A\\in\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\}\\)</span> is a <strong>minimal left ideal</strong>: it is closed under left multiplication by the algebra and contains no smaller nonzero left ideal. Its elements have the unique form</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\Psi=(\\alpha+\\beta e_1)f,\n\\qquad \\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb C_I.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Identifying <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I\\)</span> with <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(i\\)</span>, their matrices have only a first column:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho(\\Psi)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha&amp;0\\\\\\beta&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>That first column is exactly the two-component spinor. If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\rho(\\ell)=L\\)</span>, the algebraic transformation <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\Psi'=\\ell\\Psi\\)</span> stays in the ideal and reproduces <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\psi'=L\\psi\\)</span>. The bilinear <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\Psi\\Psi^\\dagger\\)</span> represents the null paravector encoded by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\psi\\psi^\\dagger\\)</span>. Choosing <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> selects a reference spin axis; it does not select a preferred direction in the physical laws.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4767-rotor-representatives-and-terminology\">\n<h1>Rotor representatives and terminology</h1>\n<p>For spatial rotations, every normalized Pauli spinor can also be represented as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\Psi=Rf\\)</span>, where <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(R\\)</span> is a unit even element of APS. An explicit matrix representative is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho(R)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha&amp;-\\beta^*\\\\\\beta&amp;\\alpha^*\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathrm{SU}(2),\n\\qquad |\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Its associated direction satisfies</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathbf n=Re_3R^\\dagger,\n\\qquad\nRfR^\\dagger=\\frac{1+\\mathbf n}{2}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Left multiplication rotates the representative: <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(R'=SR\\)</span>. Right multiplication by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\exp(-Ie_3\\chi/2)\\)</span> leaves <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf n\\)</span> unchanged but multiplies the ideal spinor by the phase <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e^{-I\\chi/2}\\)</span>. Thus the direction alone does not determine the full spinor phase.</p>\n<p>The word <em>spinor</em> is also used in APS for transformation-carrying algebra elements such as rotor or Lorentz-frame representatives. These must be distinguished from minimal-ideal spinors: a determinant-one Lorentz representative has six real parameters and encodes a frame, whereas a two-component Weyl spinor has four real components. A column spinor is not itself an invertible Lorentz transformation.</p>\n<p>Finally, the opposite chiral Lorentz spinor transforms as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\chi'=(L^\\dagger)^{-1}\\chi\\)</span>. A Dirac spinor combines both chiral types. The two-component construction here is therefore a foundation for relativistic spinors, not by itself a complete massive Dirac wavefunction.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4767-spinor-exercises\">\n<h1>Spinor exercises</h1>\n<ol>\n<li>Verify the four component formulas for <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(K\\)</span> and check directly that its Minkowski norm vanishes.</li>\n<li>Show that the normalized spinors <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((1,0)^T\\)</span>, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((0,1)^T\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((1,i)^T/\\sqrt2\\)</span> give the directions <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_3\\)</span>, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-e_3\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_2\\)</span>, respectively.</li>\n<li>Apply the boost <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(B=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{\\eta/2},e^{-\\eta/2})\\)</span> to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\psi=(1,0)^T\\)</span>. Show that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(K\\)</span> is multiplied by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e^\\eta\\)</span> and remains null.</li>\n<li>Verify <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f^2=f\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_3f=f\\)</span>. Explain why left multiplication by any APS element preserves the set <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f\\)</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Spinor pairings and projective geometry\n\n### The invariant antisymmetric pairing\n\nFor column spinors ``\\psi,\\phi\\in\\mathbb C^2``, define\n\n```math\n\\varepsilon=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\-1&0\\end{pmatrix}=i\\sigma_2,\n\\qquad\n[\\psi,\\phi]_\\varepsilon=\\psi^T\\varepsilon\\phi.\n```\n\nThis pairing is complex-bilinear and antisymmetric, not Hermitian. If ``\\psi=(\\alpha,\\beta)^T`` and ``\\phi=(\\gamma,\\delta)^T``, its value is ``\\alpha\\delta-\\beta\\gamma``. Thus it is the determinant of the matrix formed by the two columns. For every two-by-two matrix ``L``,\n\n```math\nL^T\\varepsilon L=(\\det L)\\varepsilon.\n```\n\nIn particular, simultaneous transformation by ``L\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)`` preserves this pairing. Two nonzero spinors have zero pairing exactly when they are complex-linearly dependent, hence when they define the same projective ray.\n\n### Dual and conjugate representations are different\n\nA row covector ``\\lambda`` transforms as ``\\lambda'=\\lambda L^{-1}`` so that ``\\lambda\\psi`` is invariant. The invariant antisymmetric form identifies a spinor with a covector:\n\n```math\n\\lambda_\\psi=\\psi^T\\varepsilon,\n\\qquad\n\\lambda_{L\\psi}=\\lambda_\\psi L^{-1}.\n```\n\nIn column notation the dual representation is ``L^{-T}``, and\n\n```math\nL^{-T}=\\varepsilon L\\varepsilon^{-1}.\n```\n\nThus the complex-linear dual is equivalent to the original two-dimensional representation. It is not the opposite chirality merely because it is a dual.\n\nComplex conjugation instead gives ``\\bar\\psi'=\\bar L\\bar\\psi``, where bars in this paragraph mean entrywise complex conjugation of matrices and columns, not APS Clifford conjugation. Using the antisymmetric form, define ``\\chi=\\varepsilon\\bar\\psi``; then\n\n```math\n\\chi'=\\varepsilon\\bar L\\varepsilon^{-1}\\chi\n=(L^\\dagger)^{-1}\\chi.\n```\n\nThis is the opposite-chirality transformation law used earlier. The construction from ``\\psi`` is antilinear. For the full Lorentz spin group, the two chiral representations are not equivalent by a fixed complex-linear map. Restricting to rotations gives ``(U^\\dagger)^{-1}=U``, so both have the same rotation law.\n\nThe Hermitian norm ``\\psi^\\dagger\\psi`` is rotation-invariant but not invariant under all of ``\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)``. The antisymmetric pairing, not that norm, is the invariant complex-bilinear structure on a Weyl-spinor space.\n\n### Optional extension: the projective line and the sphere\n\nThe complex projective line consists of nonzero spinors modulo nonzero complex rescaling:\n\n```math\n\\mathbb{CP}^{1}=(\\mathbb C^2\\setminus\\{0\\})/\\mathbb C^\\times,\n\\qquad [\\alpha:\\beta]=[c\\alpha:c\\beta]\\quad(c\\ne0).\n```\n\nThe map\n\n```math\n[\\psi]\\longmapsto\n\\frac{\\psi\\psi^\\dagger}{\\psi^\\dagger\\psi}\n=\\frac12(\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)\n```\n\nis independent of the chosen representative and identifies projective spinors with rank-one Hermitian projectors, or equivalently with points on the unit sphere. This is a geometric identification, not an identification of a spinor column with a spatial vector.\n\nOn the chart ``\\alpha\\ne0``, introduce ``u=\\beta/\\alpha`` and choose the normalized representative ``(1,u)^T/\\sqrt{1+|u|^2}``. Then\n\n```math\nn_1=\\frac{2\\operatorname{Re}u}{1+|u|^2},\\qquad\nn_2=\\frac{2\\operatorname{Im}u}{1+|u|^2},\\qquad\nn_3=\\frac{1-|u|^2}{1+|u|^2}.\n```\n\nConversely, ``u=(n_1+in_2)/(1+n_3)`` away from the south pole. The missing point is ``[0:1]``; the second chart ``\\beta\\ne0`` has coordinate ``v=\\alpha/\\beta=1/u`` on the overlap. In angular coordinates, ``u=e^{i\\phi}\\tan(\\theta/2)``.\n\n### Möbius transformations and phase fibers\n\nWrite a determinant-one matrix as\n\n```math\nL=\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nIts projective action is the fractional linear transformation\n\n```math\nu'=\\frac{c+du}{a+bu},\n```\n\nwith zeros of the denominator interpreted as the point at infinity. This ordering of coefficients follows from our choice ``u=\\beta/\\alpha``. The matrices ``L`` and ``-L`` have the same projective action, giving the group ``\\mathrm{PSL}(2,\\mathbb C)`` of these Möbius transformations. The unitary subgroup acts as ordinary rotations of the sphere; general elements need not preserve its round distance.\n\nNormalized spinors form the three-sphere ``S^3\\subset\\mathbb C^2``. All normalized spinors giving a fixed ``\\mathbf n`` differ by a phase ``e^{i\\chi}``, a circle ``S^1``. The map ``S^3\\to S^2`` is the **Hopf fibration**. There is no globally continuous choice of normalized spinor phase for every point of the sphere; local representatives and their phase transitions are necessary. This explains why the angular spinor formula is a local choice of representative, not a global one-to-one parametrization of spinors.\n\n### Exercises on spinor geometry\n\n1. Prove ``L^T\\varepsilon L=(\\det L)\\varepsilon`` by direct multiplication and use it to derive the covector transformation law.\n2. For ``\\psi=(1,0)^T`` and ``\\phi=(0,1)^T``, compute their antisymmetric pairing before and after the diagonal boost used earlier.\n3. Derive the stereographic coordinate formulas from the normalized outer product.\n4. Show that a rotation about the third axis acts by ``u'=e^{i\\theta}u``, while the diagonal boost acts by ``u'=e^{-\\eta}u``. Check both fix the two poles.","output":"","result":"## Spinor pairings and projective geometry\n\n### The invariant antisymmetric pairing\n\nFor column spinors ``\\psi,\\phi\\in\\mathbb C^2``, define\n\n```math\n\\varepsilon=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\-1&0\\end{pmatrix}=i\\sigma_2,\n\\qquad\n[\\psi,\\phi]_\\varepsilon=\\psi^T\\varepsilon\\phi.\n```\n\nThis pairing is complex-bilinear and antisymmetric, not Hermitian. If ``\\psi=(\\alpha,\\beta)^T`` and ``\\phi=(\\gamma,\\delta)^T``, its value is ``\\alpha\\delta-\\beta\\gamma``. Thus it is the determinant of the matrix formed by the two columns. For every two-by-two matrix ``L``,\n\n```math\nL^T\\varepsilon L=(\\det L)\\varepsilon.\n```\n\nIn particular, simultaneous transformation by ``L\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)`` preserves this pairing. Two nonzero spinors have zero pairing exactly when they are complex-linearly dependent, hence when they define the same projective ray.\n\n### Dual and conjugate representations are different\n\nA row covector ``\\lambda`` transforms as ``\\lambda'=\\lambda L^{-1}`` so that ``\\lambda\\psi`` is invariant. The invariant antisymmetric form identifies a spinor with a covector:\n\n```math\n\\lambda_\\psi=\\psi^T\\varepsilon,\n\\qquad\n\\lambda_{L\\psi}=\\lambda_\\psi L^{-1}.\n```\n\nIn column notation the dual representation is ``L^{-T}``, and\n\n```math\nL^{-T}=\\varepsilon L\\varepsilon^{-1}.\n```\n\nThus the complex-linear dual is equivalent to the original two-dimensional representation. It is not the opposite chirality merely because it is a dual.\n\nComplex conjugation instead gives ``\\bar\\psi'=\\bar L\\bar\\psi``, where bars in this paragraph mean entrywise complex conjugation of matrices and columns, not APS Clifford conjugation. Using the antisymmetric form, define ``\\chi=\\varepsilon\\bar\\psi``; then\n\n```math\n\\chi'=\\varepsilon\\bar L\\varepsilon^{-1}\\chi\n=(L^\\dagger)^{-1}\\chi.\n```\n\nThis is the opposite-chirality transformation law used earlier. The construction from ``\\psi`` is antilinear. For the full Lorentz spin group, the two chiral representations are not equivalent by a fixed complex-linear map. Restricting to rotations gives ``(U^\\dagger)^{-1}=U``, so both have the same rotation law.\n\nThe Hermitian norm ``\\psi^\\dagger\\psi`` is rotation-invariant but not invariant under all of ``\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)``. The antisymmetric pairing, not that norm, is the invariant complex-bilinear structure on a Weyl-spinor space.\n\n### Optional extension: the projective line and the sphere\n\nThe complex projective line consists of nonzero spinors modulo nonzero complex rescaling:\n\n```math\n\\mathbb{CP}^{1}=(\\mathbb C^2\\setminus\\{0\\})/\\mathbb C^\\times,\n\\qquad [\\alpha:\\beta]=[c\\alpha:c\\beta]\\quad(c\\ne0).\n```\n\nThe map\n\n```math\n[\\psi]\\longmapsto\n\\frac{\\psi\\psi^\\dagger}{\\psi^\\dagger\\psi}\n=\\frac12(\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)\n```\n\nis independent of the chosen representative and identifies projective spinors with rank-one Hermitian projectors, or equivalently with points on the unit sphere. This is a geometric identification, not an identification of a spinor column with a spatial vector.\n\nOn the chart ``\\alpha\\ne0``, introduce ``u=\\beta/\\alpha`` and choose the normalized representative ``(1,u)^T/\\sqrt{1+|u|^2}``. Then\n\n```math\nn_1=\\frac{2\\operatorname{Re}u}{1+|u|^2},\\qquad\nn_2=\\frac{2\\operatorname{Im}u}{1+|u|^2},\\qquad\nn_3=\\frac{1-|u|^2}{1+|u|^2}.\n```\n\nConversely, ``u=(n_1+in_2)/(1+n_3)`` away from the south pole. The missing point is ``[0:1]``; the second chart ``\\beta\\ne0`` has coordinate ``v=\\alpha/\\beta=1/u`` on the overlap. In angular coordinates, ``u=e^{i\\phi}\\tan(\\theta/2)``.\n\n### Möbius transformations and phase fibers\n\nWrite a determinant-one matrix as\n\n```math\nL=\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nIts projective action is the fractional linear transformation\n\n```math\nu'=\\frac{c+du}{a+bu},\n```\n\nwith zeros of the denominator interpreted as the point at infinity. This ordering of coefficients follows from our choice ``u=\\beta/\\alpha``. The matrices ``L`` and ``-L`` have the same projective action, giving the group ``\\mathrm{PSL}(2,\\mathbb C)`` of these Möbius transformations. The unitary subgroup acts as ordinary rotations of the sphere; general elements need not preserve its round distance.\n\nNormalized spinors form the three-sphere ``S^3\\subset\\mathbb C^2``. All normalized spinors giving a fixed ``\\mathbf n`` differ by a phase ``e^{i\\chi}``, a circle ``S^1``. The map ``S^3\\to S^2`` is the **Hopf fibration**. There is no globally continuous choice of normalized spinor phase for every point of the sphere; local representatives and their phase transitions are necessary. This explains why the angular spinor formula is a local choice of representative, not a global one-to-one parametrization of spinors.\n\n### Exercises on spinor geometry\n\n1. Prove ``L^T\\varepsilon L=(\\det L)\\varepsilon`` by direct multiplication and use it to derive the covector transformation law.\n2. For ``\\psi=(1,0)^T`` and ``\\phi=(0,1)^T``, compute their antisymmetric pairing before and after the diagonal boost used earlier.\n3. Derive the stereographic coordinate formulas from the normalized outer product.\n4. Show that a rotation about the third axis acts by ``u'=e^{i\\theta}u``, while the diagonal boost acts by ``u'=e^{-\\eta}u``. Check both fix the two poles.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4772-spinor-pairings-and-projective-geometry\">\n<h1>Spinor pairings and projective geometry</h1>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4772-the-invariant-antisymmetric-pairing\">\n<h1>The invariant antisymmetric pairing</h1>\n<p>For column spinors <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\psi,\\phi\\in\\mathbb C^2\\)</span>, define</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\varepsilon=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\\\-1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}=i\\sigma_2,\n\\qquad\n[\\psi,\\phi]_\\varepsilon=\\psi^T\\varepsilon\\phi.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This pairing is complex-bilinear and antisymmetric, not Hermitian. If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\psi=(\\alpha,\\beta)^T\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\phi=(\\gamma,\\delta)^T\\)</span>, its value is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\alpha\\delta-\\beta\\gamma\\)</span>. Thus it is the determinant of the matrix formed by the two columns. For every two-by-two matrix <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span>,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nL^T\\varepsilon L=(\\det L)\\varepsilon.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>In particular, simultaneous transformation by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)\\)</span> preserves this pairing. Two nonzero spinors have zero pairing exactly when they are complex-linearly dependent, hence when they define the same projective ray.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4772-dual-and-conjugate-representations-are-different\">\n<h1>Dual and conjugate representations are different</h1>\n<p>A row covector <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span> transforms as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lambda'=\\lambda L^{-1}\\)</span> so that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\lambda\\psi\\)</span> is invariant. The invariant antisymmetric form identifies a spinor with a covector:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lambda_\\psi=\\psi^T\\varepsilon,\n\\qquad\n\\lambda_{L\\psi}=\\lambda_\\psi L^{-1}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>In column notation the dual representation is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L^{-T}\\)</span>, and</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nL^{-T}=\\varepsilon L\\varepsilon^{-1}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Thus the complex-linear dual is equivalent to the original two-dimensional representation. It is not the opposite chirality merely because it is a dual.</p>\n<p>Complex conjugation instead gives <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\bar\\psi'=\\bar L\\bar\\psi\\)</span>, where bars in this paragraph mean entrywise complex conjugation of matrices and columns, not APS Clifford conjugation. Using the antisymmetric form, define <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\chi=\\varepsilon\\bar\\psi\\)</span>; then</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\chi'=\\varepsilon\\bar L\\varepsilon^{-1}\\chi\n=(L^\\dagger)^{-1}\\chi.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This is the opposite-chirality transformation law used earlier. The construction from <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\psi\\)</span> is antilinear. For the full Lorentz spin group, the two chiral representations are not equivalent by a fixed complex-linear map. Restricting to rotations gives <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((U^\\dagger)^{-1}=U\\)</span>, so both have the same rotation law.</p>\n<p>The Hermitian norm <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\psi^\\dagger\\psi\\)</span> is rotation-invariant but not invariant under all of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)\\)</span>. The antisymmetric pairing, not that norm, is the invariant complex-bilinear structure on a Weyl-spinor space.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4772-optional-extension-the-projective-line-and-the-sphere\">\n<h1>Optional extension: the projective line and the sphere</h1>\n<p>The complex projective line consists of nonzero spinors modulo nonzero complex rescaling:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathbb{CP}^{1}=(\\mathbb C^2\\setminus\\{0\\})/\\mathbb C^\\times,\n\\qquad [\\alpha:\\beta]=[c\\alpha:c\\beta]\\quad(c\\ne0).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The map</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n[\\psi]\\longmapsto\n\\frac{\\psi\\psi^\\dagger}{\\psi^\\dagger\\psi}\n=\\frac12(\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>is independent of the chosen representative and identifies projective spinors with rank-one Hermitian projectors, or equivalently with points on the unit sphere. This is a geometric identification, not an identification of a spinor column with a spatial vector.</p>\n<p>On the chart <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\alpha\\ne0\\)</span>, introduce <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(u=\\beta/\\alpha\\)</span> and choose the normalized representative <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((1,u)^T/\\sqrt{1+|u|^2}\\)</span>. Then</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nn_1=\\frac{2\\operatorname{Re}u}{1+|u|^2},\\qquad\nn_2=\\frac{2\\operatorname{Im}u}{1+|u|^2},\\qquad\nn_3=\\frac{1-|u|^2}{1+|u|^2}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Conversely, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(u=(n_1+in_2)/(1+n_3)\\)</span> away from the south pole. The missing point is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\([0:1]\\)</span>; the second chart <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\beta\\ne0\\)</span> has coordinate <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(v=\\alpha/\\beta=1/u\\)</span> on the overlap. In angular coordinates, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(u=e^{i\\phi}\\tan(\\theta/2)\\)</span>.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4772-möbius-transformations-and-phase-fibers\">\n<h1>Möbius transformations and phase fibers</h1>\n<p>Write a determinant-one matrix as</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nL=\\begin{pmatrix}a&amp;b\\\\c&amp;d\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Its projective action is the fractional linear transformation</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nu'=\\frac{c+du}{a+bu},\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>with zeros of the denominator interpreted as the point at infinity. This ordering of coefficients follows from our choice <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(u=\\beta/\\alpha\\)</span>. The matrices <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-L\\)</span> have the same projective action, giving the group <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{PSL}(2,\\mathbb C)\\)</span> of these Möbius transformations. The unitary subgroup acts as ordinary rotations of the sphere; general elements need not preserve its round distance.</p>\n<p>Normalized spinors form the three-sphere <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(S^3\\subset\\mathbb C^2\\)</span>. All normalized spinors giving a fixed <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf n\\)</span> differ by a phase <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e^{i\\chi}\\)</span>, a circle <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(S^1\\)</span>. The map <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(S^3\\to S^2\\)</span> is the <strong>Hopf fibration</strong>. There is no globally continuous choice of normalized spinor phase for every point of the sphere; local representatives and their phase transitions are necessary. This explains why the angular spinor formula is a local choice of representative, not a global one-to-one parametrization of spinors.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4772-exercises-on-spinor-geometry\">\n<h1>Exercises on spinor geometry</h1>\n<ol>\n<li>Prove <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L^T\\varepsilon L=(\\det L)\\varepsilon\\)</span> by direct multiplication and use it to derive the covector transformation law.</li>\n<li>For <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\psi=(1,0)^T\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\phi=(0,1)^T\\)</span>, compute their antisymmetric pairing before and after the diagonal boost used earlier.</li>\n<li>Derive the stereographic coordinate formulas from the normalized outer product.</li>\n<li>Show that a rotation about the third axis acts by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(u'=e^{i\\theta}u\\)</span>, while the diagonal boost acts by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(u'=e^{-\\eta}u\\)</span>. Check both fix the two poles.</li>\n</ol>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Physical interpretation and common pitfalls\n\nFor an inertial observer, a four-vector splits into a temporal scalar and a spatial vector. APS encodes this observer-dependent split as a paravector. Lorentz transformations mix the two parts, but preserve their Minkowski norm. One should not interpret the scalar part as an observer-independent absolute time.\n\nFor four-momentum, the invariant immediately gives the relativistic energy–momentum relation:\n\n```math\np\\bar p=\\frac{E^2}{c^2}-|\\mathbf p|^2=m^2c^2,\n\\qquad\nE^2=m^2c^4+c^2|\\mathbf p|^2.\n```\n\nFor light, ``m=0`` and the momentum matrix is singular. This is a geometric property of a null four-vector, not a defect of its representation.\n\nKeep the following distinctions in view:\n\n- The spatial algebra is Euclidean: ``e_j^2=+1``. The Minkowski signature belongs to the paravector norm involving Clifford conjugation.\n- A spatial vector, a bivector, and a paravector are different types of algebraic element, even though Pauli matrices can represent all of them.\n- The matrix ``i\\mathbf 1_2`` represents the spatial pseudoscalar. It is not a fourth spatial basis vector.\n- Hermitian conjugation and Clifford conjugation are different operations.\n- A Lorentz sandwich uses ``L^\\dagger``, not generally ``L^{-1}``. They agree for rotations, but not for boosts.\n- The Pauli representation realizes APS as an algebra; merely arranging four numbers in a generic matrix would not automatically preserve its multiplication.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Multiply ``\\sigma_1\\sigma_2`` and ``\\sigma_2\\sigma_1`` explicitly. Identify the corresponding bivectors in APS.\n2. Encode ``q=3+2e_1-e_3`` as a matrix. Compute its determinant, causal type, and inverse in both languages.\n3. Starting from the Pauli product identity, verify ``(\\mathbf p\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)^2=|\\mathbf p|^2\\mathbf 1_2`` and derive the eigenvalues of a paravector matrix.\n4. For a boost along ``e_3``, use ``B=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{\\eta/2},e^{-\\eta/2})`` to derive the component formulas directly from matrix multiplication.\n5. Show that ``N=(\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)/2`` satisfies ``N^2=N`` and has trace one when ``|\\mathbf n|=1``. Explain why it represents a future null paravector, despite being a projector as a matrix.\n\n**Checks:** In Exercise 2, the determinant is ``4``, the paravector is timelike, and its inverse is ``(3-2e_1+e_3)/4``. In Exercise 5, the corresponding paravector has temporal component ``1/2`` and spatial magnitude ``1/2``.\n\n## Further reading\n\nFor a fuller APS treatment, see W. E. Baylis, *Electrodynamics: A Modern Geometric Approach*. For complementary geometric-algebra viewpoints, see D. Hestenes, *Space-Time Algebra*, and C. Doran and A. Lasenby, *Geometric Algebra for Physicists*. The latter texts also develop spacetime-algebra formulations; their underlying algebra and involution conventions should not be assumed identical to the APS conventions used here.","output":"","result":"## Physical interpretation and common pitfalls\n\nFor an inertial observer, a four-vector splits into a temporal scalar and a spatial vector. APS encodes this observer-dependent split as a paravector. Lorentz transformations mix the two parts, but preserve their Minkowski norm. One should not interpret the scalar part as an observer-independent absolute time.\n\nFor four-momentum, the invariant immediately gives the relativistic energy–momentum relation:\n\n```math\np\\bar p=\\frac{E^2}{c^2}-|\\mathbf p|^2=m^2c^2,\n\\qquad\nE^2=m^2c^4+c^2|\\mathbf p|^2.\n```\n\nFor light, ``m=0`` and the momentum matrix is singular. This is a geometric property of a null four-vector, not a defect of its representation.\n\nKeep the following distinctions in view:\n\n- The spatial algebra is Euclidean: ``e_j^2=+1``. The Minkowski signature belongs to the paravector norm involving Clifford conjugation.\n- A spatial vector, a bivector, and a paravector are different types of algebraic element, even though Pauli matrices can represent all of them.\n- The matrix ``i\\mathbf 1_2`` represents the spatial pseudoscalar. It is not a fourth spatial basis vector.\n- Hermitian conjugation and Clifford conjugation are different operations.\n- A Lorentz sandwich uses ``L^\\dagger``, not generally ``L^{-1}``. They agree for rotations, but not for boosts.\n- The Pauli representation realizes APS as an algebra; merely arranging four numbers in a generic matrix would not automatically preserve its multiplication.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Multiply ``\\sigma_1\\sigma_2`` and ``\\sigma_2\\sigma_1`` explicitly. Identify the corresponding bivectors in APS.\n2. Encode ``q=3+2e_1-e_3`` as a matrix. Compute its determinant, causal type, and inverse in both languages.\n3. Starting from the Pauli product identity, verify ``(\\mathbf p\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)^2=|\\mathbf p|^2\\mathbf 1_2`` and derive the eigenvalues of a paravector matrix.\n4. For a boost along ``e_3``, use ``B=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{\\eta/2},e^{-\\eta/2})`` to derive the component formulas directly from matrix multiplication.\n5. Show that ``N=(\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)/2`` satisfies ``N^2=N`` and has trace one when ``|\\mathbf n|=1``. Explain why it represents a future null paravector, despite being a projector as a matrix.\n\n**Checks:** In Exercise 2, the determinant is ``4``, the paravector is timelike, and its inverse is ``(3-2e_1+e_3)/4``. In Exercise 5, the corresponding paravector has temporal component ``1/2`` and spatial magnitude ``1/2``.\n\n## Further reading\n\nFor a fuller APS treatment, see W. E. Baylis, *Electrodynamics: A Modern Geometric Approach*. For complementary geometric-algebra viewpoints, see D. Hestenes, *Space-Time Algebra*, and C. Doran and A. Lasenby, *Geometric Algebra for Physicists*. The latter texts also develop spacetime-algebra formulations; their underlying algebra and involution conventions should not be assumed identical to the APS conventions used here.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4760-physical-interpretation-and-common-pitfalls\">\n<h1>Physical interpretation and common pitfalls</h1>\n<p>For an inertial observer, a four-vector splits into a temporal scalar and a spatial vector. APS encodes this observer-dependent split as a paravector. Lorentz transformations mix the two parts, but preserve their Minkowski norm. One should not interpret the scalar part as an observer-independent absolute time.</p>\n<p>For four-momentum, the invariant immediately gives the relativistic energy–momentum relation:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np\\bar p=\\frac{E^2}{c^2}-|\\mathbf p|^2=m^2c^2,\n\\qquad\nE^2=m^2c^4+c^2|\\mathbf p|^2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For light, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(m=0\\)</span> and the momentum matrix is singular. This is a geometric property of a null four-vector, not a defect of its representation.</p>\n<p>Keep the following distinctions in view:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The spatial algebra is Euclidean: <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_j^2=+1\\)</span>. The Minkowski signature belongs to the paravector norm involving Clifford conjugation.</li>\n<li>A spatial vector, a bivector, and a paravector are different types of algebraic element, even though Pauli matrices can represent all of them.</li>\n<li>The matrix <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(i\\mathbf 1_2\\)</span> represents the spatial pseudoscalar. It is not a fourth spatial basis vector.</li>\n<li>Hermitian conjugation and Clifford conjugation are different operations.</li>\n<li>A Lorentz sandwich uses <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L^\\dagger\\)</span>, not generally <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L^{-1}\\)</span>. They agree for rotations, but not for boosts.</li>\n<li>The Pauli representation realizes APS as an algebra; merely arranging four numbers in a generic matrix would not automatically preserve its multiplication.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4760-exercises\">\n<h1>Exercises</h1>\n<ol>\n<li>Multiply <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\sigma_1\\sigma_2\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\sigma_2\\sigma_1\\)</span> explicitly. Identify the corresponding bivectors in APS.</li>\n<li>Encode <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(q=3+2e_1-e_3\\)</span> as a matrix. Compute its determinant, causal type, and inverse in both languages.</li>\n<li>Starting from the Pauli product identity, verify <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((\\mathbf p\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)^2=|\\mathbf p|^2\\mathbf 1_2\\)</span> and derive the eigenvalues of a paravector matrix.</li>\n<li>For a boost along <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_3\\)</span>, use <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(B=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{\\eta/2},e^{-\\eta/2})\\)</span> to derive the component formulas directly from matrix multiplication.</li>\n<li>Show that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(N=(\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)/2\\)</span> satisfies <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(N^2=N\\)</span> and has trace one when <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(|\\mathbf n|=1\\)</span>. Explain why it represents a future null paravector, despite being a projector as a matrix.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Checks:</strong> In Exercise 2, the determinant is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(4\\)</span>, the paravector is timelike, and its inverse is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((3-2e_1+e_3)/4\\)</span>. In Exercise 5, the corresponding paravector has temporal component <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1/2\\)</span> and spatial magnitude <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1/2\\)</span>.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Notebooks_Paravectors-c4760-further-reading\">\n<h1>Further reading</h1>\n<p>For a fuller APS treatment, see W. E. Baylis, <em>Electrodynamics: A Modern Geometric Approach</em>. For complementary geometric-algebra viewpoints, see D. Hestenes, <em>Space-Time Algebra</em>, and C. Doran and A. Lasenby, <em>Geometric Algebra for Physicists</em>. The latter texts also develop spacetime-algebra formulations; their underlying algebra and involution conventions should not be assumed identical to the APS conventions used here.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null}]